"{\"eval_set_result_id\":\"TravelPlannerAgent_Evaluation_test_1_1763640957.7096066\",\"eval_set_result_name\":\"TravelPlannerAgent_Evaluation_test_1_1763640957.7096066\",\"eval_set_id\":\"Evaluation_test_1\",\"eval_case_results\":[{\"eval_set_file\":\"Evaluation_test_1\",\"eval_set_id\":\"Evaluation_test_1\",\"eval_id\":\"test-1\",\"final_eval_status\":1,\"eval_metric_results\":null,\"overall_eval_metric_results\":[{\"metric_name\":\"tool_trajectory_avg_score\",\"threshold\":0.5,\"judge_model_options\":null,\"criterion\":null,\"score\":1.0,\"eval_status\":1,\"details\":{\"rubric_scores\":null}},{\"metric_name\":\"response_match_score\",\"threshold\":0.5,\"judge_model_options\":null,\"criterion\":null,\"score\":0.6016499705362405,\"eval_status\":1,\"details\":{\"rubric_scores\":null}}],\"eval_metric_result_per_invocation\":[{\"actual_invocation\":{\"invocation_id\":\"e-603fd8db-a562-480e-b1cd-4cd98d42b4bd\",\"user_content\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"Plan a 7-day cultural deep-dive travel experience in Kyoto focusing on temples, traditions, tea ceremonies, and nature.\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"user\"},\"final_response\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"This is an outstanding, well-paced itinerary that perfectly captures the cultural essence of Kyoto. As your travel consultant, I will now optimize this plan with practical, money-saving, and contingency advice to ensure a seamless cultural deep-dive.\\n\\n---\\n\\nITINERARY: This itinerary is designed to provide a thorough cultural immersion in Kyoto over seven days, balancing iconic sightseeing with deeply traditional activities like tea ceremonies and quiet temple exploration.\\n\\n# 7-Day Kyoto Cultural Deep-Dive Itinerary (Optimized)\\n\\n**Travel Theme:** Temples, Traditions, Tea Ceremonies, and Nature\\n**Best Travel Time Focus:** Autumn (October/November) or Spring (March/April)\\n\\n## Suggested Accommodation Areas (Optimization Note)\\n\\n*   **Budget Tip:** If choosing **Kyoto Station**, you gain massive savings on airport transit and daily bus/train travel time, offsetting the slightly less charming atmosphere. Look for \\\"Kyoto Tower Hotel Annex\\\" area for good mid-range options.\\n*   **Atmosphere/Mid-Range:** For Gion/Higashiyama, consider staying slightly north of the main tourist streets (near the Shijo/Sanjo Keihan line) for better value while retaining atmosphere.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Daily Itinerary Breakdown (Optimized with Tips)\\n\\n### Day 1: Arrival & Eastern Tranquility (Higashiyama Focus)\\n\\n| Activity | Dining Suggestions | Optimization/Money-Saving Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Kiyomizu-dera/Sannenzaka/Ninenzaka | Dinner: Try *Obanzai* (Kyoto home-style cooking) in a small shop off the main street in Higashiyama. | **Money Saver:** Skip buying souvenirs on Sannenzaka/Ninenzaka; prices are inflated for tourists. Buy similar goods later at Nishiki Market or Kyoto Station. **Transport Tip:** Purchase an ICOCA card immediately upon arrival for easy bus/train use. |\\n| **Evening Stroll** | | **Cultural Do's/Don'ts:** Be mindful of noise levels in the preserved Gion streets after 8 PM; residents appreciate quiet. |\\n\\n### Day 2: Zen & Golden Grandeur (Northwest Focus)\\n\\n| Activity | Dining Suggestions | Optimization/Money-Saving Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Kinkaku-ji, Ryoan-ji, Daitoku-ji | Lunch: Seek out *Sh\u014djin Ry\u014dri* (vegetarian temple cuisine) near Daitoku-ji for a traditional, health-focused, and relatively affordable lunch option compared to Kaiseki. | **Money Saver:** Entrance fees add up. Focus your day on the main three sites; Daitoku-ji is free to enter the complex, you only pay for the sub-temples you choose to enter. **Transportation:** The city bus is *essential* here. Use the **Kyoto City Bus One-Day Pass** (currently \u00a5700) if you take 3 or more bus rides today. |\\n\\n### Day 3: Nature, Spirituality, and Tea Roots (Southern Focus)\\n\\n| Activity | Dining Suggestions | Optimization/Money-Saving Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Fushimi Inari Taisha & Uji Excursion | Dinner: **Budget Alternative:** Instead of a formal dinner, make your lunch/early dinner at Nishiki Market (Day 5) or grab high-quality *Ekiben* (Bento boxes) at Kyoto Station. | **Nature/Safety:** Start Fushimi Inari *before* 7:30 AM. The path offers excellent shade, but if traveling in summer, bring significantly more water. **Backup Plan:** If Uji is too far/rainy, substitute with a visit to **T\u014dfuku-ji Temple** (near Fushimi Inari) known for its vast Zen grounds and stunning autumn colors. |\\n\\n### Day 4: Arashiyama Nature & Bamboo Serenity (Western Focus)\\n\\n| Activity | Dining Suggestions | Optimization/Money-Saving Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Tenry\u016b-ji, Bamboo Grove, Otagi Nenbutsu-ji | Lunch: **Budget Alternative:** Buy a delicious Japanese bakery item or sandwich from a local *konbini* (convenience store like 7-Eleven or Lawson) and eat it riverside for a scenic, sub-\u00a51000 lunch. | **Transportation:** The **Randen Tram** (Keifuku Electric Railroad) is a charming, historic way to approach Arashiyama and is covered by the IC card. It\u2019s much slower but adds to the \\\"nature\\\" feel. **Cultural Tip:** Otagi Nenbutsu-ji is far; factor in an extra 30 minutes travel time each way, or skip if time is tight. |\\n\\n### Day 5: Tradition Deep-Dive & Geisha District (Central/Gion Focus)\\n\\n| Activity | Dining Suggestions | Optimization/Money-Saving Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Tea Ceremony Masterclass & Nishiki Market | Dinner: Look for restaurants near **Pontocho Alley** that offer a discounted \\\"early bird\\\" or fixed-price menu before 7:00 PM. | **Cultural Do's/Don'ts:** **Tea Ceremony Booking:** Book your formal ceremony *at least* 2-3 weeks in advance, especially in high season. Western-style seating (*Ry\u016brei*) is often an option if kneeling is difficult; always inquire politely. **Nishiki Market Tip:** Taste samples are common, but do not treat them as free meals\u2014be respectful and only sample what is offered. |\\n\\n### Day 6: Reflection, Gardens, and Silver Beauty (Northern Higashiyama Focus)\\n\\n| Activity | Dining Suggestions | Optimization/Money-Saving Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Philosopher\u2019s Path, Ginkaku-ji, Nanzen-ji | Lunch: Pack snacks/water before the walk. The caf\u00e9s along the Path are scenic but expensive (\u00a51,500+ for a coffee and small snack). | **Money Saver:** Many temples here (like Nanzen-ji) have beautiful grounds you can walk for free, even if you skip paying for the main buildings. **Backup Plan (Bad Weather):** If it rains heavily, skip the Path walk. Focus your time inside the massive halls of Nanzen-ji or spend longer inside the Kyoto National Museum (near Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d, Day 7). |\\n\\n### Day 7: History & Departure\\n\\n| Activity | Dining Suggestions | Optimization/Money-Saving Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d Temple & Departure | Lunch: If departing via Shinkansen, buy your *Ekiben* at the Kyoto Station food halls; they offer fantastic regional specialties perfect for a travel meal. | **Practical Tip:** Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d does not allow photography inside. Arrive 15 minutes before opening to enjoy the 1001 Kannon statues in relative silence before the tour groups arrive. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Budget Estimate Optimization\\n\\nThe original budget is sound for mid-range travel. Here\u2019s how to shift it for savings:\\n\\n| Category | Optimized Budget (JPY) | USD Equivalent | Optimization Strategy |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Accommodation (6 Nights)** | \u00a554,000 \u2013 \u00a584,000 | $360 \u2013 $560 | Opt for clean Business Hotels (Toyoko Inn, APA) or slightly smaller rooms near a subway station instead of Ryokans/Boutique hotels. |\\n| **Local Transportation** | \u00a55,000 \u2013 \u00a57,000 | $33 \u2013 $47 | Rely heavily on the \u00a5700 Bus Pass (used 3x) and walking; minimize subway use unless needed for speed. |\\n| **Entrance Fees** | \u00a57,000 \u2013 \u00a59,000 | $47 \u2013 $60 | Focus on free areas (Fushimi Inari, Nanzen-ji grounds) and choose 5-6 key paid sites. |\\n| **Food & Drink** | \u00a536,000 \u2013 \u00a555,000 | $240 \u2013 $367 | Utilize convenience stores (Konbini) for breakfast/light lunch (\u00a51000/day average), enjoy street food, and limit fine dining to one splurge meal. |\\n| **Cultural Activities** | \u00a58,000 \u2013 \u00a512,000 | $53 \u2013 $80 | Book a cheaper, one-hour introductory tea ceremony (around \u00a54,000-\u00a56,000) instead of a formal masterclass. |\\n| **Miscellaneous/Buffer** | \u00a58,000 | $53 | Reduced buffer as daily costs are tighter. |\\n| **TOTAL OPTIMIZED BUDGET** | **\u00a5118,000 \u2013 \u00a5175,000** | **$786 \u2013 $1,167** | **Achievable for budget-conscious travelers focused on culture.** |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Packing Recommendations Specific to Destination\\n\\nKyoto demands thoughtful packing due to its mix of temple visits, walking, and variable weather depending on the season.\\n\\n1.  **Temple & Garden Ready Footwear:** Pack shoes that are **extremely easy to slip on and off**. You will do this multiple times a day. Sneakers with pull-tabs or loafers are ideal. Comfort for 15,000+ steps daily is crucial.\\n2.  **Layering for Variable Temperatures:** Evenings in Spring/Autumn get cool quickly, especially near rivers (Kamo, Shirakawa). Pack a light, packable down jacket or warm cardigan for evenings.\\n3.  **Modest Outerwear:** Bring one long-sleeved top or light scarf/shawl. While not strictly enforced at most temples, covering shoulders/knees shows respect, especially for Tea Ceremonies.\\n4.  **Small Crossbody Bag/Anti-Theft Pack:** Essential for keeping your small items secure while navigating crowded areas like Nishiki Market and Fushimi Inari.\\n5.  **Umbrella:** A collapsible umbrella is non-negotiable. It serves as rain protection during rainy seasons and often as a parasol during sunny days.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Backup Plans for Weather or Unexpected Situations\\n\\n| Situation | Primary Backup Plan | Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Heavy Rain/Typhoon** | **Museum Day:** Pivot to Kyoto National Museum (near Day 7 site) or the Kyoto International Manga Museum (Central Kyoto). | Purchase your One-Day Pass early; buses run, but subways become the fastest alternative. |\\n| **Overcrowding at Major Sites** | **Focus on Sub-Temples:** If Kinkaku-ji or Kiyomizu-dera is unbearable, head to a nearby, quieter location. | **Alternative 1:** If Fushimi Inari is too busy, spend more time hiking higher up. **Alternative 2:** If Arashiyama is packed, spend the extra time exploring the Saga-Toriimoto street uphill. |\\n| **Fushimi Inari Too Strenuous** | **Inari/Kyoto Station Area:** Spend the time exploring the **Kyoto Tower** observation deck for city views, or explore the massive underground shopping/dining complex at Kyoto Station. | This keeps you central and dry/covered. |\\n| **Tea Ceremony Cancellation** | **DIY Matcha Experience:** Purchase high-quality matcha powder in Uji (Day 3) or Nishiki Market (Day 5). Watch a YouTube tutorial and practice the basic whisking technique privately at your accommodation. | This maintains the cultural focus without the high booking cost. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Local Apps, Websites, or Resources to Download\\n\\n| Resource | Purpose | Why It\u2019s Essential |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Google Maps** | Navigation & Transit | Essential for real-time routing, showing accurate bus numbers, and walking times. |\\n| **Japan Travel by NAVITIME** | Detailed Transit Planning | Superior to Google Maps for checking specific train platform numbers, Shinkansen schedules, and calculating IC card fares. |\\n| **Google Translate (Offline Mode)** | Communication & Signage | Download the Japanese language pack. Crucial for reading small signs at temples or ordering from handwritten menus. |\\n| **Your Accommodation Booking App** | Emergency Contact & Address | Keep the hotel/Ryokan address saved in English *and* Japanese characters for taxi drivers. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Cultural Do's and Don'ts for Respectful Travel\\n\\n| DO | DON'T |\\n| :--- | :--- |\\n| **DO** carry a small, reusable bag for trash. Public bins are rare; you are expected to carry minor trash until you return to your accommodation or find a designated bin (usually near convenience stores/stations). | **DON'T** stop or touch Geiko/Maiko (Geisha/Apprentices) in Gion. They are working and deserve professional distance. Photography should only be done discreetly from afar. |\\n| **DO** use chopsticks correctly: Never stick them upright in a bowl of rice (this mimics funeral rites). | **DON'T** leave tips (*Tipping is considered rude or confusing*). Service charges are included in the bill or the price displayed. |\\n| **DO** bow slightly when saying thank you (\\\"Arigato gozaimasu\\\") to a clerk, bus driver, or host. | **DON'T** eat or drink while walking down busy streets (especially food purchased at Nishiki Market). Stop at a designated area or shop entrance to finish your item. |\\n| **DO** learn a few basic phrases (e.g., *Sumimasen* for excuse me/sorry; *Arigato* for thank you). | **DON'T** talk loudly on public transport. This is highly frowned upon; keep conversations quiet or text instead. |\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"model\"},\"intermediate_data\":{\"invocation_events\":[]},\"creation_timestamp\":1763640937.805876,\"rubrics\":null,\"app_details\":{\"agent_details\":{\"DestinationResearchAgent\":{\"name\":\"DestinationResearchAgent\",\"instructions\":\"\\n    You are a travel researcher. You will be given a destination and travel preferences, and you will research:\\n    - Best time to visit and weather patterns\\n    - Top attractions and must-see locations\\n    - Local culture, customs, and etiquette tips\\n    - Transportation options within the destination\\n    - Safety considerations and travel requirements\\n    Provide comprehensive destination insights for trip planning.\\n    \\n\\nYou are an agent. Your internal name is \\\"DestinationResearchAgent\\\". The description about you is \\\"An agent that researches travel destinations and gathers essential information\\\".\",\"tool_declarations\":[{\"function_declarations\":null,\"retrieval\":null,\"google_search_retrieval\":null,\"computer_use\":null,\"file_search\":null,\"code_execution\":null,\"enterprise_web_search\":null,\"google_maps\":null,\"google_search\":{\"exclude_domains\":null,\"blocking_confidence\":null,\"time_range_filter\":null},\"url_context\":null}]},\"ItineraryBuilderAgent\":{\"name\":\"ItineraryBuilderAgent\",\"instructions\":\"\\n    You are a professional travel planner. Using the research from \\\"destination_research\\\" output, create a detailed itinerary that includes:\\n    - Day-by-day schedule with recommended activities\\n    - Suggested accommodation areas or districts\\n    - Estimated time requirements for each activity\\n    - Meal recommendations and dining suggestions\\n    - Budget estimates for major expenses\\n    Structure it logically for easy following during the trip.\\n    \\n\\nYou are an agent. Your internal name is \\\"ItineraryBuilderAgent\\\". The description about you is \\\"An agent that creates structured travel itineraries with daily schedules\\\".\",\"tool_declarations\":[]},\"TravelOptimizerAgent\":{\"name\":\"TravelOptimizerAgent\",\"instructions\":\"\\n    You are a seasoned travel consultant. Using the itinerary from \\\"travel_itinerary\\\" output, optimize it by adding:\\n    - Money-saving tips and budget alternatives\\n    - Packing recommendations specific to the destination\\n    - Backup plans for weather or unexpected situations\\n    - Local apps, websites, or resources to download\\n    - Cultural do's and don'ts for respectful travel\\n\\n    Format the final output as:\\n    \\n    ITINERARY: This itinerary is designed to provide a thorough cultural immersion in Kyoto over seven days, balancing iconic sightseeing with deeply traditional activities like tea ceremonies and quiet temple exploration.\\n\\n# 7-Day Kyoto Cultural Deep-Dive Itinerary\\n\\n**Travel Theme:** Temples, Traditions, Tea Ceremonies, and Nature\\n**Best Travel Time Focus:** Autumn (October/November) or Spring (March/April)\\n\\n## Suggested Accommodation Areas\\n\\nFor a first-time, culture-focused trip, staying near major transport hubs or historic centers is ideal for maximizing efficiency:\\n\\n1.  **Central Kyoto (Near Karasuma/Kawaramachi):** Excellent access to both subway lines and central bus routes. Vibrant dining and shopping scene.\\n2.  **Gion/Higashiyama:** Best for atmosphere, staying near historic streets, and easy access to many famous temples. Can be slightly pricier and quieter late at night.\\n3.  **Kyoto Station Area:** Ideal for ease of arrival/departure, excellent bus connectivity, and budget options. Less atmospheric for evening walks.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Daily Itinerary Breakdown\\n\\n### Day 1: Arrival & Eastern Tranquility (Higashiyama Focus)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Arrival at KIX/ITM, Transfer to Kyoto, Check-in | 3.0 hrs | Take the Haruka Express (KIX) or Airport Limousine Bus (ITM) to Kyoto Station. |\\n| **Lunch** | Quick Lunch near Accommodation | 1.0 hr | **Dining Suggestion:** Try a quick, hearty bowl of **Ramen** near Kyoto Station (e.g., Ramen Street on the 10th floor of Isetan). |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple** | 2.0 hrs | Explore the iconic wooden stage. Remember the purification rites at the *Otowa Waterfall* at the base (choose one stream). |\\n| **Late Afternoon**| Walk Sannenzaka & Ninenzaka | 1.5 hrs | Historic preserved streets leading down from Kiyomizu-dera. Browse pottery, sweets, and souvenirs. |\\n| **Evening** | Dinner & Gion Evening Stroll | 2.5 hrs | **Dinner Suggestion:** Try *Yudofu* (boiled tofu) or traditional Kyoto cuisine in the Gion/Pontocho area. Walk the atmospheric Shirakawa Canal afterward. |\\n\\n### Day 2: Zen & Golden Grandeur (Northwest Focus)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Early Morning**| **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)** | 1.5 hrs | **CRITICAL:** Arrive right at opening (usually 9:00 AM) to witness the reflection before major crowds arrive. |\\n| **Mid-Morning**| **Ryoan-ji Temple** | 1.5 hrs | Famous for its enigmatic rock garden. Spend time meditating on the arrangements. |\\n| **Lunch** | Casual Lunch near the Temples | 1.0 hr | **Dining Suggestion:** Look for a small *soba* or *udon* shop in the area. |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Daitoku-ji Temple Complex** | 2.5 hrs | Explore the sub-temples here for deeper, quieter Zen garden appreciation. Less tourist traffic than Kinkaku-ji. |\\n| **Evening** | Relaxation & Dinner | 3.0 hrs | **Dinner Suggestion:** Explore the dining options around Kawaramachi/Shijo Street for more variety (Izakaya or casual dining). |\\n\\n### Day 3: Nature, Spirituality, and Tea Roots (Southern Focus)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Early Morning**| **Fushimi Inari Taisha** | 3.0\u20134.0 hrs | **CRITICAL:** Arrive before 8:00 AM. Hike as far up the mountain as your energy allows for the best views and quieter paths beyond the main loop. |\\n| **Lunch** | Street Food & Return | 1.0 hr | **Dining Suggestion:** Grab *Inari Sushi* or skewers from the vendors near the base of Fushimi Inari. |\\n| **Afternoon** | Travel to Uji (Optional Excursion) | 3.0 hrs | Take the JR Nara Line to Uji (approx. 20 min). This suburb is famous for the highest quality matcha. Visit **Byodo-in Temple** (featured on the 10 yen coin). |\\n| **Late Afternoon**| Uji Matcha Tasting | 1.0 hr | **Tea Focus:** Sample high-grade matcha or purchase some to take home in Uji. |\\n| **Evening** | Dinner near Kyoto Station | 2.5 hrs | **Dinner Suggestion:** Try *Okonomiyaki* (savory pancake) or explore the diverse basement *depachika* food halls for high-quality take-away items. |\\n\\n### Day 4: Arashiyama Nature & Bamboo Serenity (Western Focus)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Travel to Arashiyama & **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple** | 2.5 hrs | Take the JR Sagano Line or Randen Tram. Start at Tenry\u016b-ji to enjoy its magnificent landscape garden. |\\n| **Mid-Morning**| **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove** | 1.0 hr | Walk through the main grove. Be prepared for crowds; keep pace. |\\n| **Lunch** | Riverside Dining | 1.5 hrs | **Dining Suggestion:** Look for restaurants near the Togetsukyo Bridge offering views of the Hozugawa River. Try local tofu dishes. |\\n| **Afternoon** | Quiet Temple Exploration | 2.5 hrs | **Recommendation:** Walk further uphill past the main grove to **Otagi Nenbutsu-ji** (featuring 1200 unique, moss-covered statues carved by amateurs\u2014a truly unique cultural stop). |\\n| **Evening** | Return to Central Kyoto & Dinner | 3.0 hrs | **Dinner Suggestion:** Try *Kyo-Kaiseki* (Kyoto's refined, multi-course cuisine) if budget allows, or seek out a high-quality *tempura* specialist. |\\n\\n### Day 5: Tradition Deep-Dive & Geisha District (Central/Gion Focus)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | **Formal Tea Ceremony Masterclass** | 2.5 hrs | **MUST BOOK AHEAD.** Choose a dedicated 2-hour class focusing on ritual, whisking technique, and etiquette. Consider a place in Gion or near Nanzen-ji. |\\n| **Lunch** | Light Lunch & Shopping | 1.0 hr | **Dining Suggestion:** Quick stop for *onigiri* (rice balls) or baked goods. |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Gion District Exploration & Kennin-ji** | 2.5 hrs | Explore Hanami-koji street. Visit **Kennin-ji Temple** (Kyoto's oldest Zen temple) and view the famous twin dragons painting. |\\n| **Late Afternoon**| Nishiki Market | 1.5 hrs | Explore Kyoto\u2019s \\\"Kitchen\\\"\u2014a covered market offering regional specialties, pickles, seafood, and traditional sweets. |\\n| **Evening** | Dinner & Geiko Spotting Etiquette Practice | 3.0 hrs | **Dinner Suggestion:** Pontocho Alley offers atmospheric dining along the Kamo River. Remember: Observe *geiko/maiko* respectfully from a distance; do not stop or photograph them directly. |\\n\\n### Day 6: Reflection, Gardens, and Silver Beauty (Northern Higashiyama Focus)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | **Philosopher\u2019s Path Walk** | 1.5 hrs | Start near Ginkaku-ji and walk south. Enjoy the quiet atmosphere (especially beautiful in spring/autumn). |\\n| **Mid-Morning**| **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion)** | 1.5 hrs | Admire its beautiful moss garden and the immaculate dry sand garden (*Ginshadan*). |\\n| **Lunch** | Lunch near the Path | 1.0 hr | **Dining Suggestion:** Caf\u00e9s or simple eateries scattered near the path offer pleasant breaks. |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Nanzen-ji Temple Grounds** | 2.0 hrs | Explore the massive main gate (Sanmon) and the surprising brick aqueduct within the grounds. Some sub-temples offer quick tea services here. |\\n| **Late Afternoon**| Optional: Shoren-in Temple | 1.5 hrs | A beautiful, slightly less crowded temple complex nearby, excellent for peaceful reflection. |\\n| **Evening** | Farewell Dinner Prep | 3.0 hrs | Purchase specialty items or sake for your final night. **Dinner Suggestion:** Splurge on high-quality *Wagyu* beef or revisit your favorite neighborhood from the week. |\\n\\n### Day 7: Final Culture & Departure\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | **Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d Temple** | 1.5 hrs | A powerful cultural visit to see the 1001 statues of Kannon lined up\u2014a truly unforgettable sight. |\\n| **Late Morning**| Souvenir Shopping/Packing | 1.5 hrs | Last chance for specific Kyoto souvenirs (fans, incense, quality sweets). |\\n| **Lunch** | Last Kyoto Meal | 1.0 hr | **Dining Suggestion:** Grab *Ekiben* (station lunch box) for your journey, or enjoy a final bowl of Udon. |\\n| **Afternoon** | Travel to Airport / Next Destination | Varies | Ensure you allow plenty of time for transport back to KIX/ITM or the Shinkansen station. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Budget Estimate (Per Person, Excluding International Flights)\\n\\nThis is a mid-range budget based on comfortable accommodation and engaging in several paid cultural activities (like specialized tea ceremonies).\\n\\n| Category | Estimated Cost (JPY) | Estimated Cost (USD) (Approx. $1 = \u00a5150) | Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Accommodation (6 Nights)** | \u00a572,000 \u2013 \u00a5120,000 | $480 \u2013 $800 | Mid-range hotel/nice Ryokan (Approx. \u00a512k-\u00a520k per night) |\\n| **Local Transportation** | \u00a57,000 \u2013 \u00a510,000 | $47 \u2013 $67 | Includes IC Card top-ups, bus passes, and some local train travel. |\\n| **Entrance Fees** | \u00a58,000 \u2013 \u00a510,000 | $53 \u2013 $67 | Covers major temples (Kinkaku-ji, Kiyomizu-dera, Tenryu-ji, etc.). |\\n| **Food & Drink** | \u00a545,000 \u2013 \u00a570,000 | $300 \u2013 $467 | Budgeting \u00a57,500\u2013\u00a511,500 per day (Mix of quick meals and 1-2 nicer dinners). |\\n| **Cultural Activities** | \u00a512,000 \u2013 \u00a520,000 | $80 \u2013 $133 | Includes one formal Tea Ceremony (\u00a56,000-\u00a510,000) and minor experiences. |\\n| **Miscellaneous/Buffer** | \u00a510,000 | $67 | Small purchases, snacks, emergencies. |\\n| **TOTAL ESTIMATED BUDGET** | **\u00a5154,000 \u2013 \u00a5230,000** | **$1,027 \u2013 $1,534** | **For 7 Days, Per Person** |\\n\\n***Note on Budget:*** *Costs can be significantly reduced by staying in hostels or budget business hotels, or increased significantly by opting for high-end Kaiseki meals or luxury Ryokans.*\\n\\n    OPTIMIZATION TIPS: [your money-saving and practical tips here]\\n\\n    TRAVEL ESSENTIALS: [packing and preparation advice here]\\n\\n    BACKUP PLANS: [alternative options and contingencies here]\\n    \\n\\nYou are an agent. Your internal name is \\\"TravelOptimizerAgent\\\". The description about you is \\\"An agent that optimizes travel plans with practical advice and alternatives\\\".\",\"tool_declarations\":[]}}}},\"expected_invocation\":{\"invocation_id\":\"e-8e149827-dcc4-4e6c-8b8c-6409e9319355\",\"user_content\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"Plan a 7-day cultural deep-dive travel experience in Kyoto focusing on temples, traditions, tea ceremonies, and nature.\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"user\"},\"final_response\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"ITINERARY: This is Agent **ItineraryBuilderAgent**. I will now structure the detailed 7-day cultural deep-dive itinerary for Kyoto based on the provided research framework, enhanced with practical optimization tips.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive in Kyoto Itinerary (Optimized)\\n\\nThis itinerary focuses on minimizing travel time by grouping activities geographically, maximizing exposure to temples, deep traditions (tea/Zen), and natural beauty, while integrating essential budget, packing, and contingency planning.\\n\\n## I. Trip Overview & Logistics (Unchanged)\\n\\n*(Logistics, Budget Estimates, and Suggested Accommodation Areas remain as detailed in the original plan.)*\\n\\n---\\n\\n## II. Daily Itinerary (Optimized with Budget & Contingency Notes)\\n\\n### Day 1: Arrival & Southern Icon \u2013 The Thousand Gates\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion | **Optimization/Backup Notes** |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Arrival at KIX/ITM, transfer to Kyoto Station, check in. | Logistics | 3 hours | Grab quick *onigiri* or station bento box (*ekiben*). | **BUDGET TIP:** If flying into KIX, the **JR Haruka Express** is efficient but expensive. For savings, consider the *Limousine Bus* or the cheaper *JR Kansai Airport Rapid Service* to Osaka, then transfer to a local train to Kyoto. |\\n| **15:00** | **Fushimi Inari Taisha** Exploration. | Temple/Nature | 3.5 hours | Hike at least halfway up. | **PACKING TIP:** Bring sturdy, comfortable walking shoes\u2014the path is uneven stone/dirt. |\\n| **19:30** | Dinner near accommodation. | Local Cuisine | 1.5 hours | **Dinner:** Local *Yakiniku* or ramen. | **LOCAL APP:** Download **Google Maps** (for real-time bus tracking) and **Japan Transit Planner** (Jorudan) for all intercity routes. |\\n\\n### Day 2: Eastern Majesty \u2013 History, View, and Tea\\n\\n*Geographical Focus: Higashiyama District (East)*\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion | **Optimization/Backup Notes** |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **08:00** | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple** visit. | Iconic Temple | 1.5 hours | Must arrive early. | **BUDGET TIP:** Buy a Kyoto One-Day Bus Pass (\u00a5700) if you take 3+ bus rides today. |\\n| **09:30** | Walk down **Sannenzaka & Ninenzaka**. | Historic Streets | 1.5 hours | Sample *yatsuhashi*. | **CULTURAL DON\u2019T:** Do not block the pathway for photos on these narrow streets; tourists clogging traffic leads to resentment. |\\n| **14:00** | **Traditional Tea Ceremony Experience.** | Deep Tradition | 1.5 hours | Book in advance. | **CULTURAL DO:** Arrive silently. Observe how the host handles the utensils with reverence. |\\n| **19:00** | Dinner in Gion/Pontocho Alley. | Dining | 2 hours | Pontocho Alley dining. | **BACKUP PLAN (Gion):** If Pontocho is too crowded/expensive, pivot to Kawaramachi for a wider range of *Izakaya* options. |\\n\\n### Day 3: Northern Reflection \u2013 Zen and Gold\\n\\n*Geographical Focus: Northern Kyoto*\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion | **Optimization/Backup Notes** |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **10:00** | **Kinkaku-ji** (Golden Pavilion). | Iconic Temple | 1.5 hours | Aim for morning sunlight. | **BUDGET TIP:** Kinkaku-ji is bus-dependent; use the One-Day Pass if traveling from Central Kyoto. |\\n| **11:30** | **Ry\u014dan-ji Temple.** | Zen Garden | 1 hour | Ponder the rock garden. | **CULTURAL DO:** Sit silently at the viewing veranda at Ry\u014dan-ji. It\u2019s not about *finding* meaning, but about *being* present. |\\n| **14:00** | **Daitoku-ji Temple Complex.** | Deeper Zen | 2 hours | Explore sub-temples. | **PACKING TIP:** Bring a small notepad/sketchbook to capture impressions of the less busy gardens here. |\\n| **17:30** | Walk a segment of the **Philosopher's Path**. | Nature/Reflection | 1 hour | Enjoy late afternoon light. | **BACKUP PLAN (Weather):** If it's raining, substitute the path walk with exploring the covered shopping arcades (Teramachi/Shinkyogoku) near Karasuma Oike. |\\n\\n### Day 4: Western Serenity \u2013 Bamboo & Vegetarian Cuisine\\n\\n*Geographical Focus: Arashiyama (West)*\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion | **Optimization/Backup Notes** |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **07:00** | Travel to Arashiyama. | Transit | 45 min | | **LOCAL APP:** Use **Navitime** for highly detailed train/bus routing in Japan, especially when transferring between JR and private lines (like Hankyu for Arashiyama). |\\n| **08:00** | **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove.** | Nature Priority | 1 hour | **Crucial to arrive early.** | **CULTURAL DON\u2019T:** Do not try to climb into the small adjacent garden areas near the main path for photos\u2014it\u2019s private property. |\\n| **12:30** | **Shojin Ryori Lunch.** | Culinary Tradition | 1.5 hours | Book *shojin ryori* at Shigetsu. | **BUDGET ALTERNATIVE:** If *shojin ryori* is too costly (it can be pricey), find a small shop serving **Tofu/Yudofu** (boiled tofu), a local Arashiyama specialty, which is lighter and cheaper. |\\n| **14:30** | Scenic **Randen Tram** back east. | Experience | 2 hours | | **TRANSPORT TIP:** The Randen Tram is a beautiful, slow ride\u2014a cheaper, more scenic alternative to the Sagano train. |\\n\\n### Day 5: Shogunate Power & Culinary Heart\\n\\n*Geographical Focus: Central Kyoto*\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion | **Optimization/Backup Notes** |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **09:00** | **Nij\u014d Castle.** | History/Power | 2 hours | Nightingale Floors. | **PACKING TIP:** Bring a small coin purse (\u00a5100 and \u00a5500 coins are essential for many shrine/temple offerings and locker rentals). |\\n| **12:00** | Explore **Nishiki Market**. | Food Culture | 1.5 hours | Graze through samples. | **CULTURAL DON\u2019T:** Do not eat or drink while walking through the market. Find a small bench or designated area to consume your purchase. |\\n| **15:30** | **Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d Temple.** | Art/Worship | 1.5 hours | 1001 statues. | **BACKUP PLAN (Interior Focus):** As this is an indoor activity, it\u2019s perfect for a rainy afternoon. Photography is strictly forbidden inside. |\\n| **19:30** | Dinner in Central Kyoto. | Kyoto Specialty | 2 hours | Try *Kaiseki* or *Saba Zushi*. | **BUDGET TIP (Kaiseki):** If you want the *Kaiseki* experience without the full dinner price, look for *Kaiseki Lunch Sets* on Day 6 instead. |\\n\\n### Day 6: Deep Dive or Day Trip Exploration\\n\\n*Flex Day: Deep Dive vs. Uji Day Trip*\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion | **Optimization/Backup Notes** |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Option A (Kyoto):** Nanzen-ji/T\u014d-ji | Zen/Pagoda | Full Day | Lunch near Nanzen-ji. | **BUDGET TIP:** Visit **T\u014d-ji Temple** on the first Sunday of the month if your trip aligns, as it hosts a massive flea market (*Kobo-san* Market) which is free to enter and great for cultural browsing. |\\n| **Option B (Uji Day Trip):** | Tea Focus | Full Day | Matcha Soba/Sweets in Uji. | **TRANSPORT TIP:** Uji is easily accessible on the JR Nara Line (covered by a JR Pass if you have one). If you don't have a pass, ensure you use your IC card. |\\n| **19:30** | Farewell Dinner back in Kyoto. | Celebration | 2 hours | Splurge or revisit. | **CULTURAL DO:** Consider purchasing a small, locally crafted item (pottery, incense) near your favorite temple as a respectful memento. |\\n\\n### Day 7: Reflection and Departure\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion | **Optimization/Backup Notes** |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **09:00** | Final quiet morning walk. | Reflection | 1.5 hours | | **PACKING TIP:** If you bought liquids/perishables, use the luggage forwarding service (**Takuhaibin**) to send them ahead to the airport or your next hotel for hassle-free departure. |\\n| **10:30** | Last Matcha fix and souvenir gathering. | Last Stops | 1.5 hours | Final coffee/breakfast. | **BUDGET/TIME SAVER:** Buy standardized souvenirs (matcha powder, green tea sweets) at Kyoto Station, which is faster than browsing small shops. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## IV. Essential Recommendations for Your Cultural Deep Dive\\n\\n### 1. Money-Saving Tips & Budget Alternatives\\n\\n*   **IC Card Strategy:** Purchase an **ICOCA card** immediately upon arrival. While it doesn't offer discounts, it saves significant time compared to buying individual paper tickets for every bus/train transfer.\\n*   **Temple Hopping:** Instead of paying entry fees daily, look for temples that offer special compound passes or explore the *many* free shrines (like Yasaka Shrine).\\n*   **Lunch vs. Dinner:** Japanese fine dining (like *Kaiseki*) is dramatically cheaper at lunchtime. If you want to experience high cuisine, opt for a **Kaiseki Lunch Set** (often 40-50% cheaper than the dinner equivalent).\\n*   **Convenience Store Gems:** For extremely cheap, high-quality breakfast/snacks, utilize 7-Eleven, Lawson, or Family Mart for coffee, sandwiches, and *onigiri*.\\n\\n### 2. Packing Recommendations Specific to Kyoto\\n\\n| Item | Purpose | Cultural/Practical Context |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Comfortable Shoes** | Essential for walking 5\u201310 miles/day on uneven temple paths. | You will be removing shoes frequently; slip-on styles are highly recommended over complicated boots/laces. |\\n| **Layers (Breathable)** | Weather can shift quickly, especially in Spring/Autumn. | Temples are often cool indoors, even if sunny outside. |\\n| **Small Towel/Handkerchief** | Essential for drying hands after using public washrooms or purification fountains. | Public restrooms rarely have paper towels or air dryers. |\\n| **Portable Power Bank** | For constant use of navigation apps (Google Maps, Jorudan). | Kyoto's bus routes are complex; phone battery life is critical. |\\n| **Light Jacket/Rain Shell** | For unpredictable shoulder season weather. | A light, foldable umbrella is easily purchased locally, but a quality waterproof shell is better protection. |\\n\\n### 3. Backup Plans for Weather or Unexpected Situations\\n\\n| Situation | Primary Backup Plan | Cultural/Traditional Alternative |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Heavy Rain/Typhoon** | Focus on Central Kyoto (Nij\u014d Castle, Nishiki Market covered arcades, museums like the Kyoto National Museum). | Attend a traditional craft workshop (pottery, dyeing) held indoors. |\\n| **Extreme Heat/Humidity** | Switch to air-conditioned spaces: Kyoto Station complex (shopping/restaurants), large department stores, or underground shopping malls. | Schedule temple visits for first thing in the morning (7:30 AM) and rest during the 1 PM - 4 PM heat. |\\n| **Major Holiday Crowds (e.g., Golden Week)** | Visit Kyoto\u2019s less-trafficked Zen temples (e.g., Daitokuji sub-temples) or take the Uji day trip (Day 6) early in the morning. | Book all transportation and dinner reservations *at least* 4-6 weeks in advance. |\\n\\n### 4. Local Apps, Websites, or Resources to Download\\n\\n1.  **Google Maps:** Indispensable for real-time bus locations and routes.\\n2.  **Japan Transit Planner (Jorudan) / Navitime:** Best for checking train schedules, platform numbers, and complex transfers.\\n3.  **Google Translate:** Essential, especially for reading small menu items or complex signs at smaller, family-run shops.\\n4.  **Kyoto Bus Navi (Official):** Very helpful for understanding specific bus routes, which cover most tourist sites.\\n\\n### 5. Cultural Do's and Don'ts for Respectful Travel\\n\\n| DO | DON'T |\\n| :--- | :--- |\\n| **DO** Carry a small bag for items removed at temples (e.g., shoes, small purchases). | **DON\u2019T** Take photos of private residences or *Maiko/Geiko* without explicit, clear permission. |\\n| **DO** Say *Sumimasen* (excuse me) before squeezing past someone on a busy train or narrow path. | **DON\u2019T** Eat or drink while walking through the streets or on public transit (unless on the Shinkansen). |\\n| **DO** Use both hands when giving or receiving items (especially money, business cards, or gifts). | **DON\u2019T** Point at people, artifacts, or religious statues with your finger. Use an open hand gesture. |\\n| **DO** Bow slightly when thanking staff or receiving change. | **DON\u2019T** Leave tips\u2014it can be interpreted as confusing or even insulting. |\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"model\"},\"intermediate_data\":{\"tool_uses\":[],\"tool_responses\":[],\"intermediate_responses\":[]},\"creation_timestamp\":1763640021.829202,\"rubrics\":null,\"app_details\":null},\"eval_metric_results\":[{\"metric_name\":\"tool_trajectory_avg_score\",\"threshold\":0.5,\"judge_model_options\":null,\"criterion\":null,\"score\":1.0,\"eval_status\":1,\"details\":{\"rubric_scores\":null}},{\"metric_name\":\"response_match_score\",\"threshold\":0.5,\"judge_model_options\":null,\"criterion\":null,\"score\":0.6016499705362405,\"eval_status\":1,\"details\":{\"rubric_scores\":null}}]}],\"session_id\":\"___eval___session___1ca42519-5ca0-4554-b8a5-e61e6aec53d8\",\"session_details\":{\"id\":\"___eval___session___1ca42519-5ca0-4554-b8a5-e61e6aec53d8\",\"app_name\":\"TravelPlannerAgent\",\"user_id\":\"user\",\"state\":{\"travel_itinerary\":\"This itinerary is designed to provide a thorough cultural immersion in Kyoto over seven days, balancing iconic sightseeing with deeply traditional activities like tea ceremonies and quiet temple exploration.\\n\\n# 7-Day Kyoto Cultural Deep-Dive Itinerary\\n\\n**Travel Theme:** Temples, Traditions, Tea Ceremonies, and Nature\\n**Best Travel Time Focus:** Autumn (October/November) or Spring (March/April)\\n\\n## Suggested Accommodation Areas\\n\\nFor a first-time, culture-focused trip, staying near major transport hubs or historic centers is ideal for maximizing efficiency:\\n\\n1.  **Central Kyoto (Near Karasuma/Kawaramachi):** Excellent access to both subway lines and central bus routes. Vibrant dining and shopping scene.\\n2.  **Gion/Higashiyama:** Best for atmosphere, staying near historic streets, and easy access to many famous temples. Can be slightly pricier and quieter late at night.\\n3.  **Kyoto Station Area:** Ideal for ease of arrival/departure, excellent bus connectivity, and budget options. Less atmospheric for evening walks.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Daily Itinerary Breakdown\\n\\n### Day 1: Arrival & Eastern Tranquility (Higashiyama Focus)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Arrival at KIX/ITM, Transfer to Kyoto, Check-in | 3.0 hrs | Take the Haruka Express (KIX) or Airport Limousine Bus (ITM) to Kyoto Station. |\\n| **Lunch** | Quick Lunch near Accommodation | 1.0 hr | **Dining Suggestion:** Try a quick, hearty bowl of **Ramen** near Kyoto Station (e.g., Ramen Street on the 10th floor of Isetan). |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple** | 2.0 hrs | Explore the iconic wooden stage. Remember the purification rites at the *Otowa Waterfall* at the base (choose one stream). |\\n| **Late Afternoon**| Walk Sannenzaka & Ninenzaka | 1.5 hrs | Historic preserved streets leading down from Kiyomizu-dera. Browse pottery, sweets, and souvenirs. |\\n| **Evening** | Dinner & Gion Evening Stroll | 2.5 hrs | **Dinner Suggestion:** Try *Yudofu* (boiled tofu) or traditional Kyoto cuisine in the Gion/Pontocho area. Walk the atmospheric Shirakawa Canal afterward. |\\n\\n### Day 2: Zen & Golden Grandeur (Northwest Focus)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Early Morning**| **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)** | 1.5 hrs | **CRITICAL:** Arrive right at opening (usually 9:00 AM) to witness the reflection before major crowds arrive. |\\n| **Mid-Morning**| **Ryoan-ji Temple** | 1.5 hrs | Famous for its enigmatic rock garden. Spend time meditating on the arrangements. |\\n| **Lunch** | Casual Lunch near the Temples | 1.0 hr | **Dining Suggestion:** Look for a small *soba* or *udon* shop in the area. |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Daitoku-ji Temple Complex** | 2.5 hrs | Explore the sub-temples here for deeper, quieter Zen garden appreciation. Less tourist traffic than Kinkaku-ji. |\\n| **Evening** | Relaxation & Dinner | 3.0 hrs | **Dinner Suggestion:** Explore the dining options around Kawaramachi/Shijo Street for more variety (Izakaya or casual dining). |\\n\\n### Day 3: Nature, Spirituality, and Tea Roots (Southern Focus)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Early Morning**| **Fushimi Inari Taisha** | 3.0\u20134.0 hrs | **CRITICAL:** Arrive before 8:00 AM. Hike as far up the mountain as your energy allows for the best views and quieter paths beyond the main loop. |\\n| **Lunch** | Street Food & Return | 1.0 hr | **Dining Suggestion:** Grab *Inari Sushi* or skewers from the vendors near the base of Fushimi Inari. |\\n| **Afternoon** | Travel to Uji (Optional Excursion) | 3.0 hrs | Take the JR Nara Line to Uji (approx. 20 min). This suburb is famous for the highest quality matcha. Visit **Byodo-in Temple** (featured on the 10 yen coin). |\\n| **Late Afternoon**| Uji Matcha Tasting | 1.0 hr | **Tea Focus:** Sample high-grade matcha or purchase some to take home in Uji. |\\n| **Evening** | Dinner near Kyoto Station | 2.5 hrs | **Dinner Suggestion:** Try *Okonomiyaki* (savory pancake) or explore the diverse basement *depachika* food halls for high-quality take-away items. |\\n\\n### Day 4: Arashiyama Nature & Bamboo Serenity (Western Focus)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Travel to Arashiyama & **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple** | 2.5 hrs | Take the JR Sagano Line or Randen Tram. Start at Tenry\u016b-ji to enjoy its magnificent landscape garden. |\\n| **Mid-Morning**| **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove** | 1.0 hr | Walk through the main grove. Be prepared for crowds; keep pace. |\\n| **Lunch** | Riverside Dining | 1.5 hrs | **Dining Suggestion:** Look for restaurants near the Togetsukyo Bridge offering views of the Hozugawa River. Try local tofu dishes. |\\n| **Afternoon** | Quiet Temple Exploration | 2.5 hrs | **Recommendation:** Walk further uphill past the main grove to **Otagi Nenbutsu-ji** (featuring 1200 unique, moss-covered statues carved by amateurs\u2014a truly unique cultural stop). |\\n| **Evening** | Return to Central Kyoto & Dinner | 3.0 hrs | **Dinner Suggestion:** Try *Kyo-Kaiseki* (Kyoto's refined, multi-course cuisine) if budget allows, or seek out a high-quality *tempura* specialist. |\\n\\n### Day 5: Tradition Deep-Dive & Geisha District (Central/Gion Focus)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | **Formal Tea Ceremony Masterclass** | 2.5 hrs | **MUST BOOK AHEAD.** Choose a dedicated 2-hour class focusing on ritual, whisking technique, and etiquette. Consider a place in Gion or near Nanzen-ji. |\\n| **Lunch** | Light Lunch & Shopping | 1.0 hr | **Dining Suggestion:** Quick stop for *onigiri* (rice balls) or baked goods. |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Gion District Exploration & Kennin-ji** | 2.5 hrs | Explore Hanami-koji street. Visit **Kennin-ji Temple** (Kyoto's oldest Zen temple) and view the famous twin dragons painting. |\\n| **Late Afternoon**| Nishiki Market | 1.5 hrs | Explore Kyoto\u2019s \\\"Kitchen\\\"\u2014a covered market offering regional specialties, pickles, seafood, and traditional sweets. |\\n| **Evening** | Dinner & Geiko Spotting Etiquette Practice | 3.0 hrs | **Dinner Suggestion:** Pontocho Alley offers atmospheric dining along the Kamo River. Remember: Observe *geiko/maiko* respectfully from a distance; do not stop or photograph them directly. |\\n\\n### Day 6: Reflection, Gardens, and Silver Beauty (Northern Higashiyama Focus)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | **Philosopher\u2019s Path Walk** | 1.5 hrs | Start near Ginkaku-ji and walk south. Enjoy the quiet atmosphere (especially beautiful in spring/autumn). |\\n| **Mid-Morning**| **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion)** | 1.5 hrs | Admire its beautiful moss garden and the immaculate dry sand garden (*Ginshadan*). |\\n| **Lunch** | Lunch near the Path | 1.0 hr | **Dining Suggestion:** Caf\u00e9s or simple eateries scattered near the path offer pleasant breaks. |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Nanzen-ji Temple Grounds** | 2.0 hrs | Explore the massive main gate (Sanmon) and the surprising brick aqueduct within the grounds. Some sub-temples offer quick tea services here. |\\n| **Late Afternoon**| Optional: Shoren-in Temple | 1.5 hrs | A beautiful, slightly less crowded temple complex nearby, excellent for peaceful reflection. |\\n| **Evening** | Farewell Dinner Prep | 3.0 hrs | Purchase specialty items or sake for your final night. **Dinner Suggestion:** Splurge on high-quality *Wagyu* beef or revisit your favorite neighborhood from the week. |\\n\\n### Day 7: Final Culture & Departure\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | **Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d Temple** | 1.5 hrs | A powerful cultural visit to see the 1001 statues of Kannon lined up\u2014a truly unforgettable sight. |\\n| **Late Morning**| Souvenir Shopping/Packing | 1.5 hrs | Last chance for specific Kyoto souvenirs (fans, incense, quality sweets). |\\n| **Lunch** | Last Kyoto Meal | 1.0 hr | **Dining Suggestion:** Grab *Ekiben* (station lunch box) for your journey, or enjoy a final bowl of Udon. |\\n| **Afternoon** | Travel to Airport / Next Destination | Varies | Ensure you allow plenty of time for transport back to KIX/ITM or the Shinkansen station. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Budget Estimate (Per Person, Excluding International Flights)\\n\\nThis is a mid-range budget based on comfortable accommodation and engaging in several paid cultural activities (like specialized tea ceremonies).\\n\\n| Category | Estimated Cost (JPY) | Estimated Cost (USD) (Approx. $1 = \u00a5150) | Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Accommodation (6 Nights)** | \u00a572,000 \u2013 \u00a5120,000 | $480 \u2013 $800 | Mid-range hotel/nice Ryokan (Approx. \u00a512k-\u00a520k per night) |\\n| **Local Transportation** | \u00a57,000 \u2013 \u00a510,000 | $47 \u2013 $67 | Includes IC Card top-ups, bus passes, and some local train travel. |\\n| **Entrance Fees** | \u00a58,000 \u2013 \u00a510,000 | $53 \u2013 $67 | Covers major temples (Kinkaku-ji, Kiyomizu-dera, Tenryu-ji, etc.). |\\n| **Food & Drink** | \u00a545,000 \u2013 \u00a570,000 | $300 \u2013 $467 | Budgeting \u00a57,500\u2013\u00a511,500 per day (Mix of quick meals and 1-2 nicer dinners). |\\n| **Cultural Activities** | \u00a512,000 \u2013 \u00a520,000 | $80 \u2013 $133 | Includes one formal Tea Ceremony (\u00a56,000-\u00a510,000) and minor experiences. |\\n| **Miscellaneous/Buffer** | \u00a510,000 | $67 | Small purchases, snacks, emergencies. |\\n| **TOTAL ESTIMATED BUDGET** | **\u00a5154,000 \u2013 \u00a5230,000** | **$1,027 \u2013 $1,534** | **For 7 Days, Per Person** |\\n\\n***Note on Budget:*** *Costs can be significantly reduced by staying in hostels or budget business hotels, or increased significantly by opting for high-end Kaiseki meals or luxury Ryokans.*\",\"__llm_request_key__\":\"d74ab15b-52d1-42d7-8ad2-ed96388dff21\",\"destination_research\":\"Here is a comprehensive plan for your 7-day cultural deep-dive in Kyoto, focusing on temples, traditions, tea ceremonies, and nature.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive in Kyoto\\n\\nKyoto is renowned as the heart of traditional Japan, offering an unparalleled density of cultural sites. This itinerary balances iconic landmarks with immersive traditional experiences.\\n\\n### Best Time to Visit and Weather Patterns\\n\\nThe best times to visit Kyoto for pleasant weather and scenic beauty are **Spring (March to May)**, famous for cherry blossoms (late March to early April), and **Autumn (October to November)**, celebrated for vibrant fall foliage (peaking in November).\\n\\n*   **Autumn (Oct-Nov):** Generally considered the best weather overall, with sunny, dry, warm days transitioning to cool evenings and crisp air. November averages around 13\u00b0C (55\u00b0F).\\n*   **Spring (Mar-May):** Pleasant temperatures, though May can see more rain than autumn. Temperatures range from 11\u201317\u00b0C (52\u201364\u00b0F) in March.\\n*   **Summer (Jun-Aug):** Hot and very humid.\\n*   **Winter (Dec-Feb):** Cold, with occasional snow, offering a serene atmosphere.\\n\\n### Top Attractions & Must-See Locations (Temple Focus)\\n\\nKyoto has over 1,600 Buddhist temples and 400 Shinto shrines. This itinerary groups sites geographically to maximize your time.\\n\\n| Site Category | Recommendation | Key Cultural Highlight | Area |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Iconic Temple** | **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)** | A stunning Zen temple covered in gold leaf reflected in a pond. Visit early to avoid crowds. | Northwest |\\n| **Historic Temple** | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple** | Famous for its large wooden veranda built without nails; a UNESCO World Heritage site. | Eastern (Higashiyama) |\\n| **Shrine/Nature** | **Fushimi Inari Taisha** | Iconic Shinto shrine with thousands of vibrant red *torii* gates creating tunnels up a mountain trail. Go early for the best atmosphere. | Southern |\\n| **Zen & Gardens** | **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple** | UNESCO World Heritage site, featuring one of the city's oldest and most celebrated landscape gardens. | Western (Arashiyama) |\\n| **Zen Sanctuary** | **Daitoku-ji Temple Complex** | A secluded Zen sanctuary perfect for quiet exploration of rock gardens and traditional architecture. Less crowded than major sites. | Northern |\\n| **Tranquil Walk** | **Philosopher's Path** | A lovely stone path near quiet temples, especially beautiful during cherry blossom season. | Eastern (Higashiyama) |\\n\\n### Local Culture, Customs, and Etiquette Tips\\n\\nKyoto highly values tradition. Adhering to local customs will enhance your experience and show respect.\\n\\n*   **Temples and Shrines Etiquette:**\\n    *   **Bowing:** A slight bow upon entering temple grounds is traditional.\\n    *   **Shoes:** Be prepared to remove your shoes before entering temple buildings; wear shoes that are easy to slip on and off.\\n    *   **Photography:** Do not take photos inside buildings or where signs prohibit it. Avoid using flash.\\n    *   **Behavior:** Keep your voice down, avoid running, and refrain from eating or drinking on sacred grounds.\\n    *   **Purification Fountain (*Temizuya*):** Learn the proper way to cleanse your hands and mouth before entry (use the ladle with your right hand for the left hand, then switch, then use the water in your left palm to rinse your mouth\u2014do not touch the ladle to your lips).\\n*   **General Etiquette:**\\n    *   **Tipping:** Tipping is not customary in Japan.\\n    *   **Queuing:** Always line up orderly when waiting for buses, trains, or to enter stores.\\n    *   **Noise/Walking:** Avoid walking while eating/drinking in crowded areas and do not block narrow streets.\\n    *   **Geiko/Maiko:** If you see a *geiko* (Kyoto term for geisha) or *maiko*, admire them from a distance. Do not stop them, block their path, or touch them or their kimono.\\n\\n### Tea Ceremony Immersion\\n\\nKyoto is central to the Japanese tea ceremony (*Chanoyu*), which emphasizes harmony, respect, purity, and tranquility.\\n\\n*   **Types of Ceremony:** You can choose between a casual **Chakai** (approx. 1 hour, includes tea and sweets) or a formal **Chaji** (up to 4 hours, often includes a *kaiseki* meal).\\n*   **Etiquette:**\\n    *   Arrive on time; punctuality is crucial.\\n    *   Dress modestly.\\n    *   When offered the tea cup (*chawan*), turn it clockwise (about 90 degrees) before drinking to avoid sipping from the front presentation side.\\n    *   Remain quiet, attentive, and polite throughout.\\n    *   You may be seated on the floor (*seiza* style), but ask if cushions or Western-style seating (*Ry\u016brei* style) are available if you have difficulty sitting on the floor.\\n*   **Locations:** Seek out venues in Gion, like **Camellia Tea House**, or consider a simple ceremony at temples like **Nanzen-ji Temple** or **Shoren-in Temple** (often around \u00a5500). For a unique experience, look for sessions featuring a *geiko* or *maiko*.\\n\\n### Transportation Options\\n\\nKyoto has a very accessible public transport network, though it differs from Tokyo in its reliance on buses.\\n\\n*   **Buses:** The **best way to reach most major attractions** as the network is extensive, covering areas the trains miss (like Kinkaku-ji).\\n    *   **Tip:** You generally enter at the back and pay/exit at the front. A fixed fare often applies in the central area.\\n*   **Subway & Trains:** The subway is small (two lines: Karasuma N-S, Tozai E-W) but useful for fast travel across the city center. Local train lines (JR, Keihan, Hankyu, Randen) are excellent for reaching destinations like Arashiyama and Fushimi Inari.\\n*   **Payment:** Purchase an **IC Card** (like ICOCA) for easy, contactless payment across almost all trains and buses. A **Kyoto Bus & Subway One-Day Pass** might be cost-effective if you plan many rides.\\n*   **Walking/Biking:** Kyoto is relatively flat and great for exploring historic districts like Higashiyama on foot or by bicycle.\\n\\n### Safety Considerations and Travel Requirements\\n\\nKyoto is generally considered an **extremely safe city** with low crime rates.\\n\\n*   **General Safety:** It is safe to walk the streets at night, even solo. If you lose something, it will likely be returned.\\n    *   **Emergencies:** Dial **119** for medical emergencies/fire; **110** for police.\\n    *   **Nature Safety:** If hiking in more remote, mountainous areas, be aware of potential **Asiatic black bear sightings**, especially early morning or dusk. Travel in groups and carry noise-making items.\\n*   **Health & Documents:**\\n    *   **Water:** Tap water is safe to drink.\\n    *   **Insurance:** Travel insurance covering medical costs is highly advisable.\\n    *   **Documentation:** Always carry your passport or residency card.\\n*   **Travel Requirements:** Currently, there are generally no COVID-19 related restrictions for entry to Japan, but always check the latest official requirements before your travel date. Some popular sites in Kyoto have introduced measures to manage overtourism.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Suggested 7-Day Itinerary Framework\\n\\nThis framework mixes your interests. *Adjust based on your preferred season.*\\n\\n| Day | Theme | Focus Areas & Activities |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Day 1** | **Arrival & Eastern Tranquility** | Arrive and check in. Explore the **Higashiyama District** (Southern). Visit **Kiyomizu-dera Temple** and walk down the atmospheric *Sannenzaka* and *Ninenzaka* streets. Consider a traditional tea ceremony in the Gion/Higashiyama area. |\\n| **Day 2** | **Zen & Golden Grandeur** | **Northwest Kyoto**. Visit **Kinkaku-ji** (Golden Pavilion) first thing in the morning. Follow with **Ryoan-ji** (famous rock garden) or **Daitoku-ji** for a quieter Zen experience. |\\n| **Day 3** | **Nature & Sacred Paths** | **Southern Kyoto**. Dedicate the morning to **Fushimi Inari Taisha**, hiking through the *torii* gates. Afterward, consider a trip to Uji (a southern suburb known for tea cultivation) or a simple, late-afternoon tea ceremony near Kyoto Station. |\\n| **Day 4** | **Arashiyama Nature & Temples** | **Western Kyoto**. Travel to Arashiyama. Visit **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple** and its extensive landscape garden. Walk through the **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove** (a nature focus). Visit quieter nearby temples like **Otagi Nenbutsu-ji** for a different feel. |\\n| **Day 5** | **Tradition Deep-Dive & Tea Masterclass** | **Central/Gion**. Morning: Participate in a dedicated, longer **Tea Ceremony** focusing on the ritual's details and perhaps wear a *kimono*. Afternoon: Explore **Gion**, Kyoto\u2019s historic geisha district. Visit **Kennin-ji Temple** (one of Kyoto's oldest Zen temples) nearby. |\\n| **Day 6** | **Eastern Culture & Reflection** | **Eastern Kyoto (Northern Higashiyama)**. Walk the **Philosopher's Path**. Visit **Ginkaku-ji** (Silver Pavilion) and its unique dry sand garden. Visit **Nanzen-ji Temple** grounds, which are beautiful in all seasons and sometimes offer simple tea services. |\\n| **Day 7** | **History & Departure** | Visit a site missed or one you loved for a final morning reflection, like **Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d** (for the 1001 statues of Kannon). Do any last-minute souvenir shopping before departing. |\"},\"events\":[{\"model_version\":null,\"content\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"Plan a 7-day cultural deep-dive travel experience in Kyoto focusing on temples, traditions, tea ceremonies, and nature.\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"user\"},\"grounding_metadata\":null,\"partial\":null,\"turn_complete\":null,\"finish_reason\":null,\"error_code\":null,\"error_message\":null,\"interrupted\":null,\"custom_metadata\":null,\"usage_metadata\":null,\"live_session_resumption_update\":null,\"input_transcription\":null,\"output_transcription\":null,\"avg_logprobs\":null,\"logprobs_result\":null,\"cache_metadata\":null,\"citation_metadata\":null,\"invocation_id\":\"e-603fd8db-a562-480e-b1cd-4cd98d42b4bd\",\"author\":\"user\",\"actions\":{\"skip_summarization\":null,\"state_delta\":{},\"artifact_delta\":{},\"transfer_to_agent\":null,\"escalate\":null,\"requested_auth_configs\":{},\"requested_tool_confirmations\":{},\"compaction\":null,\"end_of_agent\":null,\"agent_state\":null,\"rewind_before_invocation_id\":null},\"long_running_tool_ids\":null,\"branch\":null,\"id\":\"f0d89e8d-2255-4514-a245-db6c5b6bea22\",\"timestamp\":1763640927.815248},{\"model_version\":\"gemini-2.5-flash-lite-preview-09-2025\",\"content\":{\"parts\