lmost all trains and buses. A **Kyoto Bus & Subway One-Day Pass** might be cost-effective if you plan many rides.\\n*   **Walking/Biking:** Kyoto is relatively flat and great for exploring historic districts like Higashiyama on foot or by bicycle.\\n\\n### Safety Considerations and Travel Requirements\\n\\nKyoto is generally considered an **extremely safe city** with low crime rates.\\n\\n*   **General Safety:** It is safe to walk the streets at night, even solo. If you lose something, it will likely be returned.\\n    *   **Emergencies:** Dial **119** for medical emergencies/fire; **110** for police.\\n    *   **Nature Safety:** If hiking in more remote, mountainous areas, be aware of potential **Asiatic black bear sightings**, especially early morning or dusk. Travel in groups and carry noise-making items.\\n*   **Health & Documents:**\\n    *   **Water:** Tap water is safe to drink.\\n    *   **Insurance:** Travel insurance covering medical costs is highly advisable.\\n    *   **Documentation:** Always carry your passport or residency card.\\n*   **Travel Requirements:** Currently, there are generally no COVID-19 related restrictions for entry to Japan, but always check the latest official requirements before your travel date. Some popular sites in Kyoto have introduced measures to manage overtourism.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Suggested 7-Day Itinerary Framework\\n\\nThis framework mixes your interests. *Adjust based on your preferred season.*\\n\\n| Day | Theme | Focus Areas & Activities |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Day 1** | **Arrival & Eastern Tranquility** | Arrive and check in. Explore the **Higashiyama District** (Southern). Visit **Kiyomizu-dera Temple** and walk down the atmospheric *Sannenzaka* and *Ninenzaka* streets. Consider a traditional tea ceremony in the Gion/Higashiyama area. |\\n| **Day 2** | **Zen & Golden Grandeur** | **Northwest Kyoto**. Visit **Kinkaku-ji** (Golden Pavilion) first thing in the morning. Follow with **Ryoan-ji** (famous rock garden) or **Daitoku-ji** for a quieter Zen experience. |\\n| **Day 3** | **Nature & Sacred Paths** | **Southern Kyoto**. Dedicate the morning to **Fushimi Inari Taisha**, hiking through the *torii* gates. Afterward, consider a trip to Uji (a southern suburb known for tea cultivation) or a simple, late-afternoon tea ceremony near Kyoto Station. |\\n| **Day 4** | **Arashiyama Nature & Temples** | **Western Kyoto**. Travel to Arashiyama. Visit **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple** and its extensive landscape garden. Walk through the **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove** (a nature focus). Visit quieter nearby temples like **Otagi Nenbutsu-ji** for a different feel. |\\n| **Day 5** | **Tradition Deep-Dive & Tea Masterclass** | **Central/Gion**. Morning: Participate in a dedicated, longer **Tea Ceremony** focusing on the ritual's details and perhaps wear a *kimono*. Afternoon: Explore **Gion**, Kyoto\u2019s historic geisha district. Visit **Kennin-ji Temple** (one of Kyoto's oldest Zen temples) nearby. |\\n| **Day 6** | **Eastern Culture & Reflection** | **Eastern Kyoto (Northern Higashiyama)**. Walk the **Philosopher's Path**. Visit **Ginkaku-ji** (Silver Pavilion) and its unique dry sand garden. Visit **Nanzen-ji Temple** grounds, which are beautiful in all seasons and sometimes offer simple tea services. |\\n| **Day 7** | **History & Departure** | Visit a site missed or one you loved for a final morning reflection, like **Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d** (for the 1001 statues of Kannon). Do any last-minute souvenir shopping before departing. |\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"model\"},\"grounding_metadata\":{\"google_maps_widget_context_token\":null,\"grounding_chunks\":[{\"maps\":null,\"retrieved_context\":null,\"web\":{\"domain\":null,\"title\":\"japanhighlights.com\",\"uri\":\"https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHabXagXNpexTklc3W9XA511lyVCkOFhapqbjY9usr-5YZ5DR8CRhM8CG9EHMg-T9c5c8vk_qdwhQHuk4cO1djKzsWxcKWlKjx0s4nFZXns