{\"maps\":null,\"retrieved_context\":null,\"web\":{\"domain\":null,\"title\":\"intentionaltravelers.com\",\"uri\":\"https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQET6RQZ8BiMqTFdZfFv1HitUTZ4fsymZOJEhlSAx8SwqFnHwiBacs1ziIE52h_hBjtL468EC2J-ieKTd4tsNZSlVEiBiOMpL0MgOR98fKn01jOhZ-uwul8mK2XsnFKeyox9llcRMFXx5V7MogeS5_cE81Vsv69b\"}}],\"grounding_supports\":[{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[0,1],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":661,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":410,\"text\":\"The best times to visit Kyoto for pleasant weather and scenic beauty are **Spring (March to May)**, famous for cherry blossoms (late March to early April), and **Autumn (October to November)**, celebrated for vibrant fall foliage (peaking in November)\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[2,3],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":808,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":664,\"text\":\"*   **Autumn (Oct-Nov):** Generally considered the best weather overall, with sunny, dry, warm days transitioning to cool evenings and crisp air\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[3],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":848,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":810,\"text\":\"November averages around 13\u00b0C (55\u00b0F)\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[1,4],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":939,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":850,\"text\":\"*   **Spring (Mar-May):** Pleasant temperatures, though May can see more rain than autumn\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[0],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":997,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":941,\"text\":\"Temperatures range from 11\u201317\u00b0C (52\u201364\u00b0F) in March\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[0,3],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":1043,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":999,\"text\":\"*   **Summer (Jun-Aug):** Hot and very humid\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[3],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":1127,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":1045,\"text\":\"*   **Winter (Dec-Feb):** Cold, with occasional snow, offering a serene atmosphere\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[5,6],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":1247,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":1187,\"text\":\"Kyoto has over 1,600 Buddhist temples and 400 Shinto shrines\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[5,7,8,9],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":1532,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":1316,\"text\":\"| Site Category | Recommendation | Key Cultural Highlight | Area |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Iconic Temple** | **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)** | A stunning Zen temple covered in gold leaf reflected in a pond\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[6,9],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":1561,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":1534,\"text\":\"Visit early to avoid crowds\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[5,8],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":1713,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":1563,\"text\":\"| Northwest |\\n| **Historic Temple** | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple** | Famous for its large wooden veranda built without nails; a UNESCO World Heritage site\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[7,9],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":1891,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":1715,\"text\":\"| Eastern (Higashiyama) |\\n| **Shrine/Nature** | **Fushimi Inari Taisha** | Iconic Shinto shrine with thousands of vibrant red *torii* gates creating tunnels up a mountain trail\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[6],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":1925,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":1893,\"text\":\"Go early for the best atmosphere\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[5,8],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":2086,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":1927,\"text\":\"| Southern |\\n| **Zen & Gardens** | **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple** | UNESCO World Heritage site, featuring one of the city's oldest and most celebrated landscape gardens\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[5],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":2266,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":2088,\"text\":\"| Western (Arashiyama) |\\n| **Zen Sanctuary** | **Daitoku-ji Temple Complex** | A secluded Zen sanctuary perfect for quiet exploration of rock gardens and traditional architecture\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[5],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":2297,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":2268,\"text\":\"Less crowded than major sites\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[6],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":2448,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":2299,\"text\":\"| Northern |\\n| **Tranquil Walk** | **Philosopher's Path** | A lovely stone path near quiet temples, especially beautiful during cherry blossom season\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[10],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":2627,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":2556,\"text\":\"Adhering to local customs will enhance your experience and show respect\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[10],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":2745,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":2630,\"text\":\"*   **Temples and Shrines Etiquette:**\\n    *   **Bowing:** A slight bow upon entering temple grounds is traditional\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[11,10],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":2876,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":2751,\"text\":\"*   **Shoes:** Be prepared to remove your shoes before entering temple buildings; wear shoes that are easy to slip on and off\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[10,12,13],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":2965,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":2882,\"text\":\"*   **Photography:** Do not take photos inside buildings or where signs prohibit it\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[10],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":2984,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":2967,\"text\":\"Avoid using flash\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[10,12,14],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":3098,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":2990,\"text\":\"*   **Behavior:** Keep your voice down, avoid running, and refrain from eating or drinking on sacred grounds\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[10],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":3377,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":3104,\"text\":\"*   **Purification Fountain (*Temizuya*):** Learn the proper way to cleanse your hands and mouth before entry (use the ladle with your right hand for the left hand, then switch, then use the water in your left palm to rinse your mouth\u2014do not touch the ladle to your lips)\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[11],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":3460,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":3379,\"text\":\"*   **General Etiquette:**\\n    *   **Tipping:** Tipping is not customary in Japan\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[11,15],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":3556,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":3466,\"text\":\"*   **Queuing:** Always line up orderly when waiting for buses, trains, or to enter stores\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[12,14],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":3669,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":3562,\"text\":\"*   **Noise/Walking:** Avoid walking while eating/drinking in crowded areas and do not block narrow streets\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[12,14,13],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":3847,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":3782,\"text\":\"Do not stop them, block their path, or touch them or their kimono\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[16],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":3995,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":3878,\"text\":\"Kyoto is central to the Japanese tea ceremony (*Chanoyu*), which emphasizes harmony, respect, purity, and tranquility\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[16,17],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":4180,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":4077,\"text\":\"1 hour, includes tea and sweets) or a formal **Chaji** (up to 4 hours, often includes a *kaiseki* meal)\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[16,18],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":4247,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":4182,\"text\":\"*   **Etiquette:**\\n    *   Arrive on time; punctuality is crucial\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[16,18],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":4271,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":4253,\"text\":\"*   Dress modestly\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[16,19],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":4420,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":4277,\"text\":\"*   When offered the tea cup (*chawan*), turn it clockwise (about 90 degrees) before drinking to avoid sipping from the front presentation side\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[16,18],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":4476,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":4426,\"text\":\"*   Remain quiet, attentive, and polite throughout\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[17],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":4654,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":4482,\"text\":\"*   You may be seated on the floor (*seiza* style), but ask if cushions or Western-style seating (*Ry\u016brei* style) are available if you have difficulty sitting on the floor\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[16,20,19],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":4840,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":4656,\"text\":\"*   **Locations:** Seek out venues in Gion, like **Camellia Tea House**, or consider a simple ceremony at temples like **Nanzen-ji Temple** or **Shoren-in Temple** (often around \u00a5500)\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[17],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":4915,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":4842,\"text\":\"For a unique experience, look for sessions featuring a *geiko* or *maiko*\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[21,22,23],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":5053,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":4946,\"text\":\"Kyoto has a very accessible public transport network, though it differs from Tokyo in its reliance on buses\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[21,24],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":5197,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":5056,\"text\":\"*   **Buses:** The **best way to reach most major attractions** as the network is extensive, covering areas the trains miss (like Kinkaku-ji)\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[21],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":5273,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":5203,\"text\":\"*   **Tip:** You generally enter at the back and pay/exit at the front\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[21],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":5321,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":5275,\"text\":\"A fixed fare often applies in the central area\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[21,23],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":5454,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":5323,\"text\":\"*   **Subway & Trains:** The subway is small (two lines: Karasuma N-S, Tozai E-W) but useful for fast travel across the city center\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[21,24,25,23],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":5576,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":5456,\"text\":\"Local train lines (JR, Keihan, Hankyu, Randen) are excellent for reaching destinations like Arashiyama and Fushimi Inari\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[21,25,23],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":5696,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":5578,\"text\":\"*   **Payment:** Purchase an **IC Card** (like ICOCA) for easy, contactless payment across almost all trains and buses\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[25,23],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":5782,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":5698,\"text\":\"A **Kyoto Bus & Subway One-Day Pass** might be cost-effective if you plan many rides\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[24,22],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":5914,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":5784,\"text\":\"*   **Walking/Biking:** Kyoto is relatively flat and great for exploring historic districts like Higashiyama on foot or by bicycle\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[13],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":6045,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":5968,\"text\":\"Kyoto is generally considered an **extremely safe city** with low crime rates\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[13],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":6122,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":6048,\"text\":\"*   **General Safety:** It is safe to walk the streets at night, even solo\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[13],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":6173,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":6124,\"text\":\"If you lose something, it will likely be returned\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[13],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":6261,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":6179,\"text\":\"*   **Emergencies:** Dial **119** for medical emergencies/fire; **110** for police\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[26],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":6470,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":6425,\"text\":\"Travel in groups and carry noise-making items\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[13],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":6545,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":6472,\"text\":\"*   **Health & Documents:**\\n    *   **Water:** Tap water is safe to drink\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[13,27],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":6629,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":6551,\"text\":\"*   **Insurance:** Travel insurance covering medical costs is highly advisable\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[28],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":6702,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":6635,\"text\":\"*   **Documentation:** Always carry your passport or residency card\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[29],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":6890,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":6704,\"text\":\"*   **Travel Requirements:** Currently, there are generally no COVID-19 related restrictions for entry to Japan, but always check the latest official requirements before your travel date\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[29],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":6966,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":6892,\"text\":\"Some popular sites in Kyoto have introduced measures to manage overtourism\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[18,20],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":7442,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":7378,\"text\":\"Consider a traditional tea ceremony in the Gion/Higashiyama area\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[6,9],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":7574,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":7509,\"text\":\"Visit **Kinkaku-ji** (Golden Pavilion) first thing in the morning\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[5,9,19],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":7668,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":7576,\"text\":\"Follow with **Ryoan-ji** (famous rock garden) or **Daitoku-ji** for a quieter Zen experience\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[7,9],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":7816,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":7734,\"text\":\"Dedicate the morning to **Fushimi Inari Taisha**, hiking through the *torii* gates\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[20,19],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":7957,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":7818,\"text\":\"Afterward, consider a trip to Uji (a southern suburb known for tea cultivation) or a simple, late-afternoon tea ceremony near Kyoto Station\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[5],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":8112,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":8050,\"text\":\"Visit **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple** and its extensive landscape garden\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[6],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":8253,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":8177,\"text\":\"Visit quieter nearby temples like **Otagi Nenbutsu-ji** for a different feel\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[16,17],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":8454,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":8333,\"text\":\"Morning: Participate in a dedicated, longer **Tea Ceremony** focusing on the ritual's details and perhaps wear a *kimono*\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[7,9],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":8590,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":8521,\"text\":\"Visit **Kennin-ji Temple** (one of Kyoto's oldest Zen temples) nearby\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[6],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":8716,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":8685,\"text\":\"Walk the **Philosopher's Path**\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[7,20],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":8899,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":8789,\"text\":\"Visit **Nanzen-ji Temple** grounds, which are beautiful in all seasons and sometimes offer simple tea services\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[5],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":9070,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":8901,\"text\":\"|\\n| **Day 7** | **History & Departure** | Visit a site missed or one you loved for a final morning reflection, like **Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d** (for the 1001 statues of Kannon)\"}}],\"retrieval_metadata\":null,\"retrieval_queries\":null,\"search_entry_point\":{\"rendered_content\":\"<style>\\n.container {\\n  align-items: center;\\n  border-radius: 8px;\\n  display: flex;\\n  font-family: Google Sans, Roboto, sans-serif;\\n  font-size: 14px;\\n  line-height: 20px;\\n  padding: 8px 12px;\\n}\\n.chip {\\n  display: inline-block;\\n  border: solid 1px;\\n  border-radius: 16px;\\n  min-width: 14px;\\n  padding: 5px 16px;\\n  text-align: center;\\n  user-select: none;\\n  margin: 0 8px;\\n  -webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent;\\n}\\n.carousel {\\n  overflow: auto;\\n  scrollbar-width: none;\\n  white-space: nowrap;\\n  margin-right: -12px;\\n}\\n.headline {\\n  display: flex;\\n  margin-right: 4px;\\n}\\n.gradient-container {\\n  position: relative;\\n}\\n.gradient {\\n  position: absolute;\\n  transform: translate(3px, -9px);\\n  height: 36px;\\n  width: 9px;\\n}\\n@media (prefers-color-scheme: light) {\\n  .container {\\n    background-color: #fafafa;\\n    box-shadow: 0 0 0 1px #0000000f;\\n  }\\n  .headline-label {\\n    color: #1f1f1f;\\n  }\\n  .chip {\\n    background-color: #ffffff;\\n    border-color: #d2d2d2;\\n    color: #5e5e5e;\\n    text-decoration: none;\\n  }\\n  .chip:hover {\\n    background-color: #f2f2f2;\\n  }\\n  .chip:focus {\\n    background-color: #f2f2f2;\\n  }\\n  .chip:active {\\n    background-color: #d8d8d8;\\n    border-color: #b6b6b6;\\n  }\\n  .logo-dark {\\n    display: none;\\n  }\\n  .gradient {\\n    background: linear-gradient(90deg, #fafafa 15%, #fafafa00 100%);\\n  }\\n}\\n@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {\\n  .container {\\n    background-color: #1f1f1f;\\n    box-shadow: 0 0 0 1px #ffffff26;\\n  }\\n  .headline-label {\\n    color: #fff;\\n  }\\n  .chip {\\n    background-color: #2c2c2c;\\n    border-color: #3c4043;\\n    color: #fff;\\n    text-decoration: none;\\n  }\\n  .chip:hover {\\n    background-color: #353536;\\n  }\\n  .chip:focus {\\n    background-color: #353536;\\n  }\\n  .chip:active {\\n    background-color: #464849;\\n    border-color: #53575b;\\n  }\\n  .logo-light {\\n    display: none;\\n  }\\n  .gradient {\\n    background: linear-gradient(90deg, #1f1f1f 15%, #1f1f1f00 100%);\\n  }\\n}\\n</style>\\n<div class=\\\"container\\\">\\n  <div class=\\\"headline\\\">\\n    <svg class=\\\"logo-light\\\" width=\\\"18\\\" height=\\\"18\\\" viewBox=\\\"9 9 35 35\\\" fill=\\\"none\\\" xmlns=\\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\\">\\n      <path fill-rule=\\\"evenodd\\\" clip-rule=\\\"evenodd\\\" d=\\\"M42.8622 27.0064C42.8622 25.7839 42.7525 24.6084 42.5487 23.4799H26.3109V30.1568H35.5897C35.1821 32.3041 33.9596 34.1222 32.1258 35.3448V39.6864H37.7213C40.9814 36.677 42.8622 32.2571 42.8622 27.0064V27.0064Z\\\" fill=\\\"#4285F4\\\"/>\\n      <path fill-rule=\\\"evenodd\\\" clip-rule=\\\"evenodd\\\" d=\\\"M26.3109 43.8555C30.9659 43.8555 34.8687 42.3195 37.7213 39.6863L32.1258 35.3447C30.5898 36.3792 28.6306 37.0061 26.3109 37.0061C21.8282 37.0061 18.0195 33.9811 16.6559 29.906H10.9194V34.3573C13.7563 39.9841 19.5712 43.8555 26.3109 43.8555V43.8555Z\\\" fill=\\\"#34A853\\\"/>\\n      <path fill-rule=\\\"evenodd\\\" clip-rule=\\\"evenodd\\\" d=\\\"M16.6559 29.8904C16.3111 28.8559 16.1074 27.7588 16.1074 26.6146C16.1074 25.4704 16.3111 24.3733 16.6559 23.3388V18.8875H10.9194C9.74388 21.2072 9.06992 23.8247 9.06992 26.6146C9.06992 29.4045 9.74388 32.022 10.9194 34.3417L15.3864 30.8621L16.6559 29.8904V29.8904Z\\\" fill=\\\"#FBBC05\\\"/>\\n      <path fill-rule=\\\"evenodd\\\" clip-rule=\\\"evenodd\\\" d=\\\"M26.3109 16.2386C28.85 16.2386 31.107 17.1164 32.9095 18.8091L37.8466 13.8719C34.853 11.082 30.9659 9.3736 26.3109 9.3736C19.5712 9.3736 13.7563 13.245 10.9194 18.8875L16.6559 23.3388C18.0195 19.2636 21.8282 16.2386 26.3109 16.2386V16.2386Z\\\" fill=\\\"#EA4335\\\"/>\\n    </svg>\\n    <svg class=\\\"logo-dark\\\" width=\\\"18\\\" height=\\\"18\\\" viewBox=\\\"0 0 48 48\\\" xmlns=\\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\\">\\n      <circle cx=\\\"24\\\" cy=\\\"23\\\" fill=\\\"#FFF\\\" r=\\\"22\\\"/>\\n      <path d=\\\"M33.76 34.26c2.75-2.56 4.49-6.37 4.49-11.26 0-.89-.08-1.84-.29-3H24.01v5.99h8.03c-.4 2.02-1.5 3.56-3.07 4.56v.75l3.91 2.97h.88z\\\" fill=\\\"#4285F4\\\"/>\\n      <path d=\\\"M15.58 25.77A8.845 8.845 0 0 0 24 31.86c1.92 0 3.62-.46 4.97-1.31l4.79 3.71C31.14 36.7 27.65 38 24 38c-5.93 0-11.01-3.4-13.45-8.36l.17-1.01 4.06-2.85h.8z\\\" fill=\\\"#34A853\\\"/>\\n      <path d=\\\"M15.59 20.21a8.864 8.864 0 0 0 0 5.58l-5.03 3.86c-.98-2-1.53-4.25-1.53-6.64 0-2.39.55-4.64 1.53-6.64l1-.22 3.81 2.98.22 1.08z\\\" fill=\\\"#FBBC05\\\"/>\\n      <path d=\\\"M24 14.14c2.11 0 4.02.75 5.52 1.98l4.36-4.36C31.22 9.43 27.81 8 24 8c-5.93 0-11.01 3.4-13.45 8.36l5.03 3.85A8.86 8.86 0 0 1 24 14.14z\\\" fill=\\\"#EA4335\\\"/>\\n    </svg>\\n    <div class=\\\"gradient-container\\\"><div class=\\\"gradient\\\"></div></div>\\n  </div>\\n  <div class=\\\"carousel\\\">\\n    <a class=\\\"chip\\\" href=\\\"https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGMXURdKOP5AJBiwqStB3__M0lav_2Ngn-ZwnO3mIuLZW65J0SJhnDy25dcIV3Fsp4LD12oh7rZoGF2DjUdV5driCB44IF2DtKvF49pk4KP2vI6NdaLwbfH_I93BZkvhw0t5Sp8RUUmAXukR1iGy89sHPQ7snKGPeeHLchNdVjeGrt0EhdWT6WSn5nBDb9h3eF-zcAmwsgwRMs6NuWUZ01nVbjLYjtSGTj21NfKD-Yl\\\">must-see temples and cultural sites in Kyoto</a>\\n    <a class=\\\"chip\\\" href=\\\"https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQERCoXNpSO34xulkc1T_QdFudDYpdjDPGiXVP5goMbvNi4bqPdQpXdojn6rKKfVVNrEsjY5Y-NlfI83MinsndSY6-xl_YtCFxZHAXXxils-JnPSmTqfBXM_eLALoA34NWyWAH--GYdpeMq_cmD4UqOoXWP_BzH7kQ5_bBclaGBcyOtqIbfgGhyx4o__sUnjcNs9g0mP_YasJe5bnSNsrPXQ6mNF\\\">best time to visit Kyoto weather</a>\\n    <a class=\\\"chip\\\" href=\\\"https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHosLg31PNUSgeeHHVJ8CgSI0d6y_6zg0uCIS1ciulNuR6TOUbBW6VY22nLbMwFZ3Gna3mLUOAzwzg4MMuaDjWxzI-24qB2d2KNdOCdIMT1tZGNNZDWZnAkrn0pkX902j7nJhiE-R7XleDDfQ02mEukhqu-nSJGbA1uG2c3h0IktAFz_C5f71-JWdL6ROMMeNIHlGSL7KTOoleEstDKy6rEP52Ztg9jYYvXZwa2XQ==\\\">Kyoto tea ceremony etiquette and locations</a>\\n    <a class=\\\"chip\\\" href=\\\"https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHiDR6G6fwMHOmvXUfNBKJhTpeqivOBAQqLDvIyIPUmzwuAEK9GTGtICHQKFlJdcjjb9zOxnHqmeLsblt2je4GaZH08FUBpzChDBNvwTFuhqF0WrydgcRbE8YclicaTC5YO8HbLBbPdZWiID_IxCnB-tADfGv9k0i9ep4q5cFVcgxe6I5JOnnB_jvx3eZBF6DVQsscV6EGF7frmkP8M-y_1EadttSUtcg==\\\">Kyoto safety and travel requirements</a>\\n    <a class=\\\"chip\\\" href=\\\"https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEPsX4SEc8ZekflVTM4nzUrQb5avlGhgEE6k6SYTB8gmQjAfsM2-dLE3NifVniR611Up9M1-E8uJmeJcFP40v0PLDa_B2Bm9Qo0Z9BB9DEZ5SMd08IVOiTE48NRiIx5hGBprZaQ8qv8z1csfb7KJYrg0RlkYEoOMxWcBk1WeeRmeKTtN3RaGT8zbO6pFWSEO-tDea4nTBe1VCkP39vhKcd6lSU=\\\">transportation options in Kyoto</a>\\n    <a class=\\\"chip\\\" href=\\\"https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEJ6iTg3Ne2pE6nTps2sTOPexMFYA8zHZHEIWEXsOWS5nRBbyiY1RYQsrsccjIvOEEKxElbkLY3usXi5POqI5bm2L7gd9kNA__AE04zbIUp0zkB7zGFibGPsf2ALOcQjOViIYAuTYeEd9okg_aEb1ogm2UUpPBfZJMTvPCz3mdIhUuQzuNzuRli9Gt-o-6U_ofr6N3MWqGNHG5jawptkUHL_uUUq8w5BGz3\\\">Kyoto local customs and etiquette tips</a>\\n  </div>\\n</div>\\n\",\"sdk_blob\":null},\"source_flagging_uris\":null,\"web_search_queries\":[\"best time to visit Kyoto weather\",\"must-see temples and cultural sites in Kyoto\",\"Kyoto tea ceremony etiquette and locations\",\"Kyoto local customs and etiquette tips\",\"transportation options in Kyoto\",\"Kyoto safety and travel requirements\"]},\"partial\":null,\"turn_complete\":null,\"finish_reason\":\"STOP\",\"error_code\":null,\"error_message\":null,\"interrupted\":null,\"custom_metadata\":{\"__llm_request_key__\":\"10a617ea-c600-4ef3-ba56-6d5ef05ca02f\"},\"usage_metadata\":{\"cache_tokens_details\":null,\"cached_content_token_count\":null,\"candidates_token_count\":2319,\"candidates_tokens_details\":null,\"prompt_token_count\":142,\"prompt_tokens_details\":[{\"modality\":\"TEXT\",\"token_count\":142}],\"thoughts_token_count\":null,\"tool_use_prompt_token_count\":142,\"tool_use_prompt_tokens_details\":[{\"modality\":\"TEXT\",\"token_count\":142}],\"total_token_count\":2603,\"traffic_type\":null},\"live_session_resumption_update\":null,\"input_transcription\":null,\"output_transcription\":null,\"avg_logprobs\":null,\"logprobs_result\":null,\"cache_metadata\":null,\"citation_metadata\":null,\"invocation_id\":\"e-603fd8db-a562-480e-b1cd-4cd98d42b4bd\",\"author\":\"DestinationResearchAgent\",\"actions\":{\"skip_summarization\":null,\"state_delta\":{\"__llm_request_key__\":\"10a617ea-c600-4ef3-ba56-6d5ef05ca02f\",\"destination_research\":\"Here is a comprehensive plan for your 7-day cultural deep-dive in Kyoto, focusing on temples, traditions, tea ceremonies, and nature.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive in Kyoto\\n\\nKyoto is renowned as the heart of traditional Japan, offering an unparalleled density of cultural sites. This itinerary balances iconic landmarks with immersive traditional experiences.\\n\\n### Best Time to Visit and Weather Patterns\\n\\nThe best times to visit Kyoto for pleasant weather and scenic beauty are **Spring (March to May)**, famous for cherry blossoms (late March to early April), and **Autumn (October to November)**, celebrated for vibrant fall foliage (peaking in November).\\n\\n*   **Autumn (Oct-Nov):** Generally considered the best weather overall, with sunny, dry, warm days transitioning to cool evenings and crisp air. November averages around 13\u00b0C (55\u00b0F).\\n*   **Spring (Mar-May):** Pleasant temperatures, though May can see more rain than autumn. Temperatures range from 11\u201317\u00b0C (52\u201364\u00b0F) in March.\\n*   **Summer (Jun-Aug):** Hot and very humid.\\n*   **Winter (Dec-Feb):** Cold, with occasional snow, offering a serene atmosphere.\\n\\n### Top Attractions & Must-See Locations (Temple Focus)\\n\\nKyoto has over 1,600 Buddhist temples and 400 Shinto shrines. This itinerary groups sites geographically to maximize your time.\\n\\n| Site Category | Recommendation | Key Cultural Highlight | Area |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Iconic Temple** | **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)** | A stunning Zen temple covered in gold leaf reflected in a pond. Visit early to avoid crowds. | Northwest |\\n| **Historic Temple** | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple** | Famous for its large wooden veranda built without nails; a UNESCO World Heritage site. | Eastern (Higashiyama) |\\n| **Shrine/Nature** | **Fushimi Inari Taisha** | Iconic Shinto shrine with thousands of vibrant red *torii* gates creating tunnels up a mountain trail. Go early for the best atmosphere. | Southern |\\n| **Zen & Gardens** | **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple** | UNESCO World Heritage site, featuring one of the city's oldest and most celebrated landscape gardens. | Western (Arashiyama) |\\n| **Zen Sanctuary** | **Daitoku-ji Temple Complex** | A secluded Zen sanctuary perfect for quiet exploration of rock gardens and traditional architecture. Less crowded than major sites. | Northern |\\n| **Tranquil Walk** | **Philosopher's Path** | A lovely stone path near quiet temples, especially beautiful during cherry blossom season. | Eastern (Higashiyama) |\\n\\n### Local Culture, Customs, and Etiquette Tips\\n\\nKyoto highly values tradition. Adhering to local customs will enhance your experience and show respect.\\n\\n*   **Temples and Shrines Etiquette:**\\n    *   **Bowing:** A slight bow upon entering temple grounds is traditional.\\n    *   **Shoes:** Be prepared to remove your shoes before entering temple buildings; wear shoes that are easy to slip on and off.\\n    *   **Photography:** Do not take photos inside buildings or where signs prohibit it. Avoid using flash.\\n    *   **Behavior:** Keep your voice down, avoid running, and refrain from eating or drinking on sacred grounds.\\n    *   **Purification Fountain (*Temizuya*):** Learn the proper way to cleanse your hands and mouth before entry (use the ladle with your right hand for the left hand, then switch, then use the water in your left palm to rinse your mouth\u2014do not touch the ladle to your lips).\\n*   **General Etiquette:**\\n    *   **Tipping:** Tipping is not customary in Japan.\\n    *   **Queuing:** Always line up orderly when waiting for buses, trains, or to enter stores.\\n    *   **Noise/Walking:** Avoid walking while eating/drinking in crowded areas and do not block narrow streets.\\n    *   **Geiko/Maiko:** If you see a *geiko* (Kyoto term for geisha) or *maiko*, admire them from a distance. Do not stop them, block their path, or touch them or their kimono.\\n\\n### Tea Ceremony Immersion\\n\\nKyoto is central to the Japanese tea ceremony (*Chanoyu*), which emphasizes harmony, respect, purity, and tranquility.\\n\\n*   **Types of Ceremony:** You can choose between a casual **Chakai** (approx. 1 hour, includes tea and sweets) or a formal **Chaji** (up to 4 hours, often includes a *kaiseki* meal).\\n*   **Etiquette:**\\n    *   Arrive on time; punctuality is crucial.\\n    *   Dress modestly.\\n    *   When offered the tea cup (*chawan*), turn it clockwise (about 90 degrees) before drinking to avoid sipping from the front presentation side.\\n    *   Remain quiet, attentive, and polite throughout.\\n    *   You may be seated on the floor (*seiza* style), but ask if cushions or Western-style seating (*Ry\u016brei* style) are available if you have difficulty sitting on the floor.\\n*   **Locations:** Seek out venues in Gion, like **Camellia Tea House**, or consider a simple ceremony at temples like **Nanzen-ji Temple** or **Shoren-in Temple** (often around \u00a5500). For a unique experience, look for sessions featuring a *geiko* or *maiko*.\\n\\n### Transportation Options\\n\\nKyoto has a very accessible public transport network, though it differs from Tokyo in its reliance on buses.\\n\\n*   **Buses:** The **best way to reach most major attractions** as the network is extensive, covering areas the trains miss (like Kinkaku-ji).\\n    *   **Tip:** You generally enter at the back and pay/exit at the front. A fixed fare often applies in the central area.\\n*   **Subway & Trains:** The subway is small (two lines: Karasuma N-S, Tozai E-W) but useful for fast travel across the city center. Local train lines (JR, Keihan, Hankyu, Randen) are excellent for reaching destinations like Arashiyama and Fushimi Inari.\\n*   **Payment:** Purchase an **IC Card** (like ICOCA) for easy, contactless payment across almost all trains and buses. A **Kyoto Bus & Subway One-Day Pass** might be cost-effective if you plan many rides.\\n*   **Walking/Biking:** Kyoto is relatively flat and great for exploring historic districts like Higashiyama on foot or by bicycle.\\n\\n### Safety Considerations and Travel Requirements\\n\\nKyoto is generally considered an **extremely safe city** with low crime rates.\\n\\n*   **General Safety:** It is safe to walk the streets at night, even solo. If you lose something, it will likely be returned.\\n    *   **Emergencies:** Dial **119** for medical emergencies/fire; **110** for police.\\n    *   **Nature Safety:** If hiking in more remote, mountainous areas, be aware of potential **Asiatic black bear sightings**, especially early morning or dusk. Travel in groups and carry noise-making items.\\n*   **Health & Documents:**\\n    *   **Water:** Tap water is safe to drink.\\n    *   **Insurance:** Travel insurance covering medical costs is highly advisable.\\n    *   **Documentation:** Always carry your passport or residency card.\\n*   **Travel Requirements:** Currently, there are generally no COVID-19 related restrictions for entry to Japan, but always check the latest official requirements before your travel date. Some popular sites in Kyoto have introduced measures to manage overtourism.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Suggested 7-Day Itinerary Framework\\n\\nThis framework mixes your interests. *Adjust based on your preferred season.*\\n\\n| Day | Theme | Focus Areas & Activities |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Day 1** | **Arrival & Eastern Tranquility** | Arrive and check in. Explore the **Higashiyama District** (Southern). Visit **Kiyomizu-dera Temple** and walk down the atmospheric *Sannenzaka* and *Ninenzaka* streets. Consider a traditional tea ceremony in the Gion/Higashiyama area. |\\n| **Day 2** | **Zen & Golden Grandeur** | **Northwest Kyoto**. Visit **Kinkaku-ji** (Golden Pavilion) first thing in the morning. Follow with **Ryoan-ji** (famous rock garden) or **Daitoku-ji** for a quieter Zen experience. |\\n| **Day 3** | **Nature & Sacred Paths** | **Southern Kyoto**. Dedicate the morning to **Fushimi Inari Taisha**, hiking through the *torii* gates. Afterward, consider a trip to Uji (a southern suburb known for tea cultivation) or a simple, late-afternoon tea ceremony near Kyoto Station. |\\n| **Day 4** | **Arashiyama Nature & Temples** | **Western Kyoto**. Travel to Arashiyama. Visit **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple** and its extensive landscape garden. Walk through the **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove** (a nature focus). Visit quieter nearby temples like **Otagi Nenbutsu-ji** for a different feel. |\\n| **Day 5** | **Tradition Deep-Dive & Tea Masterclass** | **Central/Gion**. Morning: Participate in a dedicated, longer **Tea Ceremony** focusing on the ritual's details and perhaps wear a *kimono*. Afternoon: Explore **Gion**, Kyoto\u2019s historic geisha district. Visit **Kennin-ji Temple** (one of Kyoto's oldest Zen temples) nearby. |\\n| **Day 6** | **Eastern Culture & Reflection** | **Eastern Kyoto (Northern Higashiyama)**. Walk the **Philosopher's Path**. Visit **Ginkaku-ji** (Silver Pavilion) and its unique dry sand garden. Visit **Nanzen-ji Temple** grounds, which are beautiful in all seasons and sometimes offer simple tea services. |\\n| **Day 7** | **History & Departure** | Visit a site missed or one you loved for a final morning reflection, like **Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d** (for the 1001 statues of Kannon). Do any last-minute souvenir shopping before departing. |\"},\"artifact_delta\":{},\"transfer_to_agent\":null,\"escalate\":null,\"requested_auth_configs\":{},\"requested_tool_confirmations\":{},\"compaction\":null,\"end_of_agent\":null,\"agent_state\":null,\"rewind_before_invocation_id\":null},\"long_running_tool_ids\":null,\"branch\":null,\"id\":\"ce11f9fe-c9c6-437b-85bc-bddc75ce7aaa\",\"timestamp\":1763640927.816265},{\"model_version\":\"gemini-2.5-flash-lite-preview-09-2025\",\"content\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"This itinerary is designed to provide a thorough cultural immersion in Kyoto over seven days, balancing iconic sightseeing with deeply traditional activities like tea ceremonies and quiet temple exploration.\\n\\n# 7-Day Kyoto Cultural Deep-Dive Itinerary\\n\\n**Travel Theme:** Temples, Traditions, Tea Ceremonies, and Nature\\n**Best Travel Time Focus:** Autumn (October/November) or Spring (March/April)\\n\\n## Suggested Accommodation Areas\\n\\nFor a first-time, culture-focused trip, staying near major transport hubs or historic centers is ideal for maximizing efficiency:\\n\\n1.  **Central Kyoto (Near Karasuma/Kawaramachi):** Excellent access to both subway lines and central bus routes. Vibrant dining and shopping scene.\\n2.  **Gion/Higashiyama:** Best for atmosphere, staying near historic streets, and easy access to many famous temples. Can be slightly pricier and quieter late at night.\\n3.  **Kyoto Station Area:** Ideal for ease of arrival/departure, excellent bus connectivity, and budget options. Less atmospheric for evening walks.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Daily Itinerary Breakdown\\n\\n### Day 1: Arrival & Eastern Tranquility (Higashiyama Focus)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Arrival at KIX/ITM, Transfer to Kyoto, Check-in | 3.0 hrs | Take the Haruka Express (KIX) or Airport Limousine Bus (ITM) to Kyoto Station. |\\n| **Lunch** | Quick Lunch near Accommodation | 1.0 hr | **Dining Suggestion:** Try a quick, hearty bowl of **Ramen** near Kyoto Station (e.g., Ramen Street on the 10th floor of Isetan). |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple** | 2.0 hrs | Explore the iconic wooden stage. Remember the purification rites at the *Otowa Waterfall* at the base (choose one stream). |\\n| **Late Afternoon**| Walk Sannenzaka & Ninenzaka | 1.5 hrs | Historic preserved streets leading down from Kiyomizu-dera. Browse pottery, sweets, and souvenirs. |\\n| **Evening** | Dinner & Gion Evening Stroll | 2.5 hrs | **Dinner Suggestion:** Try *Yudofu* (boiled tofu) or traditional Kyoto cuisine in the Gion/Pontocho area. Walk the atmospheric Shirakawa Canal afterward. |\\n\\n### Day 2: Zen & Golden Grandeur (Northwest Focus)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Early Morning**| **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)** | 1.5 hrs | **CRITICAL:** Arrive right at opening (usually 9:00 AM) to witness the reflection before major crowds arrive. |\\n| **Mid-Morning**| **Ryoan-ji Temple** | 1.5 hrs | Famous for its enigmatic rock garden. Spend time meditating on the arrangements. |\\n| **Lunch** | Casual Lunch near the Temples | 1.0 hr | **Dining Suggestion:** Look for a small *soba* or *udon* shop in the area. |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Daitoku-ji Temple Complex** | 2.5 hrs | Explore the sub-temples here for deeper, quieter Zen garden appreciation. Less tourist traffic than Kinkaku-ji. |\\n| **Evening** | Relaxation & Dinner | 3.0 hrs | **Dinner Suggestion:** Explore the dining options around Kawaramachi/Shijo Street for more variety (Izakaya or casual dining). |\\n\\n### Day 3: Nature, Spirituality, and Tea Roots (Southern Focus)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Early Morning**| **Fushimi Inari Taisha** | 3.0\u20134.0 hrs | **CRITICAL:** Arrive before 8:00 AM. Hike as far up the mountain as your energy allows for the best views and quieter paths beyond the main loop. |\\n| **Lunch** | Street Food & Return | 1.0 hr | **Dining Suggestion:** Grab *Inari Sushi* or skewers from the vendors near the base of Fushimi Inari. |\\n| **Afternoon** | Travel to Uji (Optional Excursion) | 3.0 hrs | Take the JR Nara Line to Uji (approx. 20 min). This suburb is famous for the highest quality matcha. Visit **Byodo-in Temple** (featured on the 10 yen coin). |\\n| **Late Afternoon**| Uji Matcha Tasting | 1.0 hr | **Tea Focus:** Sample high-grade matcha or purchase some to take home in Uji. |\\n| **Evening** | Dinner near Kyoto Station | 2.5 hrs | **Dinner Suggestion:** Try *Okonomiyaki* (savory pancake) or explore the diverse basement *depachika* food halls for high-quality take-away items. |\\n\\n### Day 4: Arashiyama Nature & Bamboo Serenity (Western Focus)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Travel to Arashiyama & **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple** | 2.5 hrs | Take the JR Sagano Line or Randen Tram. Start at Tenry\u016b-ji to enjoy its magnificent landscape garden. |\\n| **Mid-Morning**| **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove** | 1.0 hr | Walk through the main grove. Be prepared for crowds; keep pace. |\\n| **Lunch** | Riverside Dining | 1.5 hrs | **Dining Suggestion:** Look for restaurants near the Togetsukyo Bridge offering views of the Hozugawa River. Try local tofu dishes. |\\n| **Afternoon** | Quiet Temple Exploration | 2.5 hrs | **Recommendation:** Walk further uphill past the main grove to **Otagi Nenbutsu-ji** (featuring 1200 unique, moss-covered statues carved by amateurs\u2014a truly unique cultural stop). |\\n| **Evening** | Return to Central Kyoto & Dinner | 3.0 hrs | **Dinner Suggestion:** Try *Kyo-Kaiseki* (Kyoto's refined, multi-course cuisine) if budget allows, or seek out a high-quality *tempura* specialist. |\\n\\n### Day 5: Tradition Deep-Dive & Geisha District (Central/Gion Focus)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | **Formal Tea Ceremony Masterclass** | 2.5 hrs | **MUST BOOK AHEAD.** Choose a dedicated 2-hour class focusing on ritual, whisking technique, and etiquette. Consider a place in Gion or near Nanzen-ji. |\\n| **Lunch** | Light Lunch & Shopping | 1.0 hr | **Dining Suggestion:** Quick stop for *onigiri* (rice balls) or baked goods. |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Gion District Exploration & Kennin-ji** | 2.5 hrs | Explore Hanami-koji street. Visit **Kennin-ji Temple** (Kyoto's oldest Zen temple) and view the famous twin dragons painting. |\\n| **Late Afternoon**| Nishiki Market | 1.5 hrs | Explore Kyoto\u2019s \\\"Kitchen\\\"\u2014a covered market offering regional specialties, pickles, seafood, and traditional sweets. |\\n| **Evening** | Dinner & Geiko Spotting Etiquette Practice | 3.0 hrs | **Dinner Suggestion:** Pontocho Alley offers atmospheric dining along the Kamo River. Remember: Observe *geiko/maiko* respectfully from a distance; do not stop or photograph them directly. |\\n\\n### Day 6: Reflection, Gardens, and Silver Beauty (Northern Higashiyama Focus)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | **Philosopher\u2019s Path Walk** | 1.5 hrs | Start near Ginkaku-ji and walk south. Enjoy the quiet atmosphere (especially beautiful in spring/autumn). |\\n| **Mid-Morning**| **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion)** | 1.5 hrs | Admire its beautiful moss garden and the immaculate dry sand garden (*Ginshadan*). |\\n| **Lunch** | Lunch near the Path | 1.0 hr | **Dining Suggestion:** Caf\u00e9s or simple eateries scattered near the path offer pleasant breaks. |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Nanzen-ji Temple Grounds** | 2.0 hrs | Explore the massive main gate (Sanmon) and the surprising brick aqueduct within the grounds. Some sub-temples offer quick tea services here. |\\n| **Late Afternoon**| Optional: Shoren-in Temple | 1.5 hrs | A beautiful, slightly less crowded temple complex nearby, excellent for peaceful reflection. |\\n| **Evening** | Farewell Dinner Prep | 3.0 hrs | Purchase specialty items or sake for your final night. **Dinner Suggestion:** Splurge on high-quality *Wagyu* beef or revisit your favorite neighborhood from the week. |\\n\\n### Day 7: Final Culture & Departure\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes & Dining Suggestions |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | **Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d Temple** | 1.5 hrs | A powerful cultural visit to see the 1001 statues of Kannon lined up\u2014a truly unforgettable sight. |\\n| **Late Morning**| Souvenir Shopping/Packing | 1.5 hrs | Last chance for specific Kyoto souvenirs (fans, incense, quality sweets). |\\n| **Lunch** | Last Kyoto Meal | 1.0 hr | **Dining Suggestion:** Grab *Ekiben* (station lunch box) for your journey, or enjoy a final bowl of Udon. |\\n| **Afternoon** | Travel to Airport / Next Destination | Varies | Ensure you allow plenty of time for transport back to KIX/ITM or the Shinkansen station. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Budget Estimate (Per Person, Excluding International Flights)\\n\\nThis is a mid-range budget based on comfortable accommodation and engaging in several paid cultural activities (like specialized tea ceremonies).\\n\\n| Category | Estimated Cost (JPY) | Estimated Cost (USD) (Approx. $1 = \u00a5150) | Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Accommodation (6 Nights)** | \u00a572,000 \u2013 \u00a5120,000 | $480 \u2013 $800 | Mid-range hotel/nice Ryokan (Approx. \u00a512k-\u00a520k per night) |\\n| **Local Transportation** | \u00a57,000 \u2013 \u00a510,000 | $47 \u2013 $67 | Includes IC Card top-ups, bus passes, and some local train travel. |\\n| **Entrance Fees** | \u00a58,000 \u2013 \u00a510,000 | $53 \u2013 $67 | Covers major temples (Kinkaku-ji, Kiyomizu-dera, Tenryu-ji, etc.). |\\n| **Food & Drink** | \u00a545,000 \u2013 \u00a570,000 | $300 \u2013 $467 | Budgeting \u00a57,500\u2013\u00a511,500 per day (Mix of quick meals and 1-2 nicer dinners). |\\n| **Cultural Activities** | \u00a512,000 \u2013 \u00a520,000 | $80 \u2013 $133 | Includes one formal Tea Ceremony (\u00a56,000-\u00a510,000) and minor experiences. |\\n| **Miscellaneous/Buffer** | \u00a510,000 | $67 | Small purchases, snacks, emergencies. |\\n| **TOTAL ESTIMATED BUDGET** | **\u00a5154,000 \u2013 \u00a5230,000** | **$1,027 \u2013 $1,534** | **For 7 Days, Per Person** |\\n\\n***Note on Budget:*** *Costs can be significantly reduced by staying in hostels or budget business hotels, or increased significantly by opting for high-end Kaiseki meals or luxury Ryokans.*\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"model\"},\"grounding_metadata\":null,\"partial\":null,\"turn_complete\":null,\"finish_reason\":\"STOP\",\"error_code\":null,\"error_message\":null,\"interrupted\":null,\"custom_metadata\":{\"__llm_request_key__\":\"676cf197-a6ac-46d0-b5de-1c7c74614e9c\"},\"usage_metadata\":{\"cache_tokens_details\":null,\"cached_content_token_count\":null,\"candidates_token_count\":2740,\"candidates_tokens_details\":null,\"prompt_token_count\":2413,\"prompt_tokens_details\":[{\"modality\":\"TEXT\",\"token_count\":2413}],\"thoughts_token_count\":null,\"tool_use_prompt_token_count\":null,\"tool_use_prompt_tokens_details\":null,\"total_token_count\":5153,\"traffic_type\":null},\"live_session_resumption_update\":null,\"input_transcription\":null,\"output_transcription\":null,\"avg_logprobs\":null,\"logprobs_result\":null,\"cache_metadata\":null,\"citation_metadata\":null,\"invocation_id\":\"e-603fd8db-a562-480e-b1cd-4cd98d42b4bd\",\"author\":\"ItineraryBuilderAgent\",\"actions\":{\"skip_summarization\":null,\"state_delta\":{\"__llm_request_key__\":\"676cf197-a6ac-46d0-b5de-1c7c74614e9c\",\"travel_itinerary\":\"This itinerary is designed to provide a thorough cultural immersion in Kyoto over seven days, balancing iconic sightseeing with deeply traditional activities like tea ceremonies and quiet temple exploration.\\n\\n# 7-Day Kyoto Cultural Deep-Dive Itinerary\\n\\n**Travel Theme:** Temples, Traditions, Tea Ceremonies, and Nature\\n**Best Travel Time Focus:** Autumn (October/November) or Spring (March/April)\\n\\n## Suggested Accommodation Areas\\n\\nFor a first-time, culture-focused trip, staying near major transport hubs or historic centers is 